"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/scan.12\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/scan.14\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/scan.16\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/scan.18\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/scan.20\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/scan.22\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/scan.24\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_0\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_1\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_2\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_3\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_4\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_5\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_6\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_7\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_8\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_9\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_10\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_11\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_12\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_13\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_14\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_15\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_16\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_17\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_18\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_19\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_20\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/funding_boundary0.10_21\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_0\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_1\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_2\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_3\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_4\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_5\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_6\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_7\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_8\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_9\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_10\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_11\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_12\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_13\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_14\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_15\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_16\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_17\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_18\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_19\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_20\"","\"reverse/base/margin/h0.15/bisect0.20_21\"","\"sensitivity/ar_days/60\"","\"sensitivity/ar_days/75\"","\"sensitivity/ar_days/90\"","\"sensitivity/ar_days/105\"","\"sensitivity/ar_days/120\"","\"sensitivity/maintenance_fraction/.01\"","\"sensitivity/maintenance_fraction/.02\"","\"sensitivity/maintenance_fraction/.03\"","\"sensitivity/maintenance_fraction/.04\"","\"sensitivity/cc_due_postentry_month/3\"","\"sensitivity/cc_due_postentry_month/5\"","\"sensitivity/cc_due_postentry_month/12\"","\"sensitivity/cc_due_postentry_month/60\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_project_cash/0\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_project_cash/3\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_project_cash/6\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_project_cash/12\"","\"sensitivity/cash_cycles/4\"","\"sensitivity/cash_cycles/8\"","\"sensitivity/cash_cycles/16\"","\"sensitivity/cash_cycles/32\"","\"sensitivity/debt_rate/.08\"","\"sensitivity/debt_rate/.10\"","\"sensitivity/debt_rate/.14\"","\"sensitivity/minimum_cash/.01\"","\"sensitivity/minimum_cash/.02\"","\"sensitivity/minimum_cash/.05\"","\"sensitivity/growth_fixed_fraction/.10\"","\"sensitivity/growth_fixed_fraction/.15\"","\"sensitivity/growth_fixed_fraction/.25\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.08/.10\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.08/.145\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.08/.17\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.08/.20\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.12/.10\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.12/.145\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.12/.17\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.12/.20\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.20/.10\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.20/.145\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.20/.17\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.20/.20\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.30/.10\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.30/.145\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.30/.17\"","\"sensitivity/growth_margin/.30/.20\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.12/.02\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.12/.035\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.12/.05\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.15/.02\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.15/.035\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.15/.05\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.18/.02\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.18/.035\"","\"sensitivity/terminal_k_g/.18/.05\"","\"arguments\"","\"overrides\"","\".01\"","\".04\"","\"One complete expanded path pergzipJSONLline. No dropped events, decimaldigits, failedcandidates or rootbranches. All identical expanded content paths deduplicated by content SHA256, preserving every argument request and alias.\"","\"SHA256 canonicalfullpath excludingid; canonicalusesUTF8ensure_asciifalse, sortkeys andcompactseparators.\"","\"label\"","\"ar_days/60\"","\"ar_days/75\"","\"ar_days/90\"","\"ar_days/105\"","\"ar_days/120\"","\"maintenance_fraction/.01\"","\"maintenance_fraction/.02\"","\"maintenance_fraction/.03\"","\"maintenance_fraction/.04\"","\"cc_due_postentry_month/3\"","\"cc_due_postentry_month/5\"","\"cc_due_postentry_month/12\"","\"cc_due_postentry_month/60\"","\"terminal_project_cash/0\"","\"terminal_project_cash/3\"","\"terminal_project_cash/6\"","\"terminal_project_cash/12\"","\"cash_cycles/4\"","\"cash_cycles/8\"","\"cash_cycles/16\"","\"cash_cycles/32\"","\"debt_rate/.08\"","\"debt_rate/.10\"","\"debt_rate/.14\"","\"minimum_cash/.01\"","\"minimum_cash/.02\"","\"minimum_cash/.05\"","\"growth_fixed_fraction/.10\"","\"growth_fixed_fraction/.15\"","\"growth_fixed_fraction/.25\"","\"growth_margin/.08/.10\"","\"growth_margin/.08/.145\"","\"growth_margin/.08/.17\"","\"growth_margin/.08/.20\"","\"growth_margin/.12/.10\"","\"growth_margin/.12/.145\"","\"growth_margin/.12/.17\"","\"growth_margin/.12/.20\"","\"growth_margin/.20/.10\"","\"growth_margin/.20/.145\"","\"growth_margin/.20/.17\"","\"growth_margin/.20/.20\"","\"growth_margin/.30/.10\"","\"growth_margin/.30/.145\"","\"growth_margin/.30/.17\"","\"growth_margin/.30/.20\"","\"terminal_k_g/.12/.02\"","\"terminal_k_g/.12/.035\"","\"terminal_k_g/.12/.05\"","\"terminal_k_g/.15/.02\"","\"terminal_k_g/.15/.035\"","\"terminal_k_g/.15/.05\"","\"terminal_k_g/.18/.02\"","\"terminal_k_g/.18/.035\"","\"terminal_k_g/.18/.05\"","\"# Repono Limited: v3.0 deep research\\n\\n**BSE SME · REPONO · 544463 · INE15WN01014**  \\n**Information cutoff: 20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata. Market reference: 19 October 2026.**  \\n₹ crore unless stated; share counts are whole shares. V = verified disclosure, C = calculation, M = management statement, A = analyst assumption. This is research, not a trade instruction.\\n\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**Hold — unresolved; a specialist-services expansion and capital-allocation watchlist, not an evidence-backed compounder or an eligible new-purchase case.** Repono operates customers' petroleum, petrochemical and packaging facilities and provides warehousing and consulting. Parent revenue has grown at 26.64% over FY21–FY26, and specialist qualifications and multiyear contracts are useful strengths. But competition is price-led, cash realization was weak before the IPO, and expansion into owning/building a terminal changes the funding burden. The apparent bargain at the secondary ₹73.90 reference price cannot yet be underwritten: BQS, GQS and IAS are **NS**, with explicit coverage and bounds below. The FY26 negative CFO is **not all operating burn** because it includes large FD placements, but those deposits are not verified free cash either. Reconciled accounts, a bank/proceeds map, committed terminal funding and observable collections, rather than another customer announcement, would change this assessment. [AR26] [IPO] [CALL25]\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n| Item | Research basis |\\n|---|---|\\n| Exact issuer | Repono Limited, formerly Repono Private Limited; CIN L74999MH2017PLC290217; incorporated 6 February 2017; BSE SME listing 4 August 2025. |\\n| Security | ISIN INE15WN01014, BSE 544463. Official 19 October interop master independently corroborates issuer, 10,279,200 issued shares and 1,200-share board lot. It does not validate a trade price or current surveillance status. |\\n| Valuation reference | ₹73.90, IIFL dated 19 October close, corroborated by Trendlyne. Investing shows ₹74.10 against a delayed intraday timestamp; discrepancy retained. BSE bhavcopy not retrieved. Not an executable quote. |\\n| Equity value | ₹75.9633 crore at current issued shares; no inference that historical weighted-average shares equal today's claims. |\\n| Financial perimeter | Consolidated FY26 retained alongside standalone. Parent operations used for the cash/valuation model because new terminal subsidiary has no revenue and consolidated negative NCI / Saudi scope are unresolved. This is not a disguised consolidated fair value. |\\n| Accounting | Indian GAAP Accounting Standards **AS**, including AS25 interim reporting, not Ind AS. Rental costs remain operating expense; no fabricated Ind AS116 liability. |\\n| Horizon / hurdles | Five years after the analytical entry date; 15% nominal INR equity hurdle, before investor taxes. Terminal capital charge separately assumed 15%, tested at 12%/18%; it is not an estimated issuer WACC. |\\n| Implementation | Illustrative ₹5 lakh reference, not a suitable allocation recommendation. Five lots cost ₹443,400, six ₹532,080 before charges. |\\n\\nIdentity and lot are supported by [IPO] and [MASTER19]. Financial scope is from [AR26]; price limitations are from [MARKET19].\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Evidence and resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | Conditional | No substantiated serious integrity or minority violation located; related finance and treasury deployment require oversight. Reconcile related-party approvals, terms and recoveries; contrary serious evidence would change this gate. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Audits are unmodified, but FY25 cash-flow vintages, FY26 negative NCI / foreign-subsidiary perimeter and issuance-asset cash classifications are unresolved. Obtain auditor/issuer reconciliation of scope, equity/NCI, prior CFO classification and issuance assets, supported by bank/proceeds mapping. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | No reported default; FD availability, CC renewal and full terminal funding cannot be underwritten without assumed releases/rescue. Obtain facilities, maturities/covenants, FD liens, release calendar, committed financing and the complete project cash budget. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | Identity and lot verified, but complete official 20/60-day tape, restrictions, spread and depth are not verified for the reference position. Verify exchange tape and executable depth/restrictions for the lot-adjusted position; a last trade is not assured execution. |\\n\\n**Confidence by subject:** High on exact issuer/issued shares/lot and transcription of identified figures; Medium on historical parent operations, audits and governance assessment; Medium on underlying recent revenue expansion, but Low on a price/volume decomposition; Low on full incremental capital returns, consolidated claim reconciliation, available liquidity, terminal financing and investment valuation. **Overall investment confidence: Low**, the weakest material area, not an average. Unmodified opinions are positive evidence, not an assurance of fraud absence or future financing. Gross profit and manufacturing inventory days are NA as comparable service-business measures; separately identified contract assets and complete lease-commitment schedules are ND, not assumed zero.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\nThe actual Master Prompt v3.0 weights are used without renormalization. Each of the nine quality factors and four IAS factors is assigned once and reused. Unknowns are not assigned zero, five, a peer average or a capped point score.\\n\\n| Measure | Raw numeric score | Final | Coverage | Raw mathematical missing-data bound | Applicable cap | Post-cap bound |\\n|---|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| BQS | NS | NS | 80.00% | 4.360–6.160 | Not applied | 4.360–6.160 |\\n| GQS | NS | NS | 85.00% | 5.400–6.750 | 5 | 5.000–5.000 |\\n| IAS | NS | NS | 20.00% | 2.240–7.640 | Not applied | 2.240–7.640 |\\n\\nBQS known contribution = 0.20×5 + 0.20×5 + 0.12×8 + 0.10×5 + 0.10×3 + 0.08×5 = **4.16**, with CAP/BAL weight 0.20 missing. Hence 4.16 + [0.20, 2.00] = **4.36–6.16**, not a point estimate. GQS known contribution is **5.25**, missing CAP weight 0.15, giving **5.40–6.75** before the persistent-CASH≤3 cap of 5. Its capped bound collapses to 5–5, **but the score remains NS**. This does not prove a GQS of 5.\\n\\nIAS directly scoreable coverage is 20% (D only). Its BQS input is the **post-cap BQS bound**, weighted once, producing **2.24–7.64**, not an IAS estimate. Effective nested evidence coverage is 40%, disclosed separately rather than substituted for direct coverage. There is **no governance or funding numeric override** because G1/G3 have not failed on sufficient evidence. G2 and G4 holds independently prohibit a favorable investment classification. Persistent poor cash realization, not merely an IPO FD placement or a single capex year, supports the GQS cap.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence, contrary evidence and rationale |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | 5 | Medium | Current main and IFC opinions are unmodified; promoters held 72.47%, with no reported encumbrance at March 2026.; Financing to divested Repono Green remains ₹3.1357 crore; director pay is ₹1.2 crore and capital-allocation transparency needs improvement. Contrary: No substantiated diversion or minority abuse was found. Unresolved financial/proceeds inconsistencies create G2/G3 holds, not a fabricated fraud finding. [AR26, IPO, AR24, SHP26] |\\n| MOAT | 5 | Medium | Specialist hazardous-product handling, trained teams and multiyear contracts support qualification and switching friction.; Management describes L1 retendering, lost contracts and a low bid strike rate; FY25 largest customer was 34.37% and top five 82.45%. Contrary: Prestigious clients and qualification do not establish pricing power; public tenders reopen competition. [IPO, CALL25, DEEPAK] |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | Chosen parent FY21–FY26 revenue ₹20.0357211→₹65.2668 crore gives five-year CAGR in the 20–30% band.; FY25–FY26 revenue rose 27.69%; the new terminal subsidiary had zero revenue, so it did not create reported turnover growth. Contrary: FY21–FY23 contraction makes the three-year recovery rate misleading. Price/volume and customer cohorts are ND. Starting score 8, no trajectory adjustment, Medium-confidence ceiling 8. [AR21, AR22, AR23, AR24, IPO, AR26, HY25] |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | Existing O&M contracts usually run three to five years; management stated ₹240 crore remaining backlog over about 36 months in November 2025.; A ₹9.6 crore three-year Deepak contract is documented; terminal agreement and railway plan approval provide access, not operating cash returns. Contrary: Backlog is not independently reconciled and contractual guarantees/termination rights were not audited. Retenders, energy transition and project execution constrain duration. [CALL25, DEEPAK, RIL26, RAIL26] |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | Core trade-plus-fixed-capital incremental proxies can be calculated, but exclude some operating balances and do not establish project-cohort returns.; FY26 parent cash capex was ₹8.8755 crore and total loans/advances ₹6.689 crore; Mathura cost, funding, tariffs and returns remain ND. Contrary: Existing profitable asset-light services are a positive. Aggregate accounting returns cannot establish incremental terminal returns. NS, rather than a made-up weak or neutral score. [AR26, IPO, CALL25] |\\n| CASH | 3 | Medium | Pre-IPO FY24–FY25 restated CFO was ₹0.303426 crore versus PAT ₹9.331204 crore; original FY22–FY23 cash realization was also weak.; Receivables older than six months rose ₹0.2306→₹2.5422 crore, alongside the material ₹1.17 crore export credit-note reversal. Contrary: FY26 reported CFO includes FD placements and subsidiary finance. These are separately diagnosed, not labeled clean normalization. The growth cap reflects repeated weak realization, not capex or FD placement alone. [AR22, AR23, AR24, IPO, AR26, HY25] |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | Parent cash is ₹0.0337 crore versus debt ₹9.1252 crore; IPO FDs ₹16.6535 crore and other FDs ₹3.0231 crore lack a verified availability/lien map.; CC renewal, undrawn facilities and committed terminal finance are ND; bank guarantees ₹4.8425 crore and GST dispute ₹0.2460 crore add contingent exposure. Contrary: Debt/EBITDA is moderate and CARO reports no default. Evidence is insufficient for resilient funding or an evidenced unfinanceable default: NS and G3 Hold. [AR26, RIL26, CALL25] |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | November 2025 FY26 revenue indication of ₹65–66 crore compares with ₹65.2668 crore operating revenue achieved.; The terminal award anticipated by December 2025 was announced in May 2026; IPO equipment/packing/software allocations remained materially unspent in March. Contrary: New contracts and revenue were delivered. Saudi commercial revenue is not reconciled against the March 2026 aspiration. Mixed execution, not automatic failure. [CALL25, AR26, RIL26, SAUDI26] |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium | Operating EBIT grows, but other income ₹1.1864 crore contains interest and is not entirely service earnings.; FY26 current-share parent EPS ₹6.4026 is below restated FY25 ₹6.8654 on the pre-IPO, bonus-adjusted base; dilution offsets total-profit growth. Contrary: No arbitrary addback of the export reversal or all finance charges. IPO expense charged to premium is not operating profit. Total-profit growth is real, but per-share quality is mixed. [IPO, AR26, HY25] |\\n| R | NS | Low | Strict treasury modeling breaches the cash floor before entry; no ordinary return is reported after a breach.; Conditional base survival requires unverified FD access plus optional bounded equity and debt, so its calculated CAGR is not a supported base return. Contrary: Conditional bull success is not a base investment return. R remains NS regardless of a displayed conditional CAGR. [AR26, MODEL] |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | Aged receivables, the export reversal and customer concentration create a credible loss-of-collection path.; Linked operating, CC nonrenewal and project stresses overwhelm bounded equity; existing shareholders face dilution and uncertain recovery. Contrary: Profitable contracts and unmodified audits are positives. Material impairment risk is not a forecast default probability or a liquidation valuation. [AR26, IPO, MODEL] |\\n| P | NS | Low | Price, growth and margin roots are solved only along explicitly cash-feasible paths, with repriced rescue shares.; Project economics, FD access and official trade-price verification remain incomplete; conditional roots are not market-certified expectations. Contrary: Simple P/E looks modest but does not establish a margin of safety. No defensible expectation score follows while funding and claims remain unresolved. [MODEL, MARKET19, RIL26] |\\n| L | NS | Low | The official 19 October lot is 1,200 shares, worth ₹88,680 at the secondary price; ₹5 lakh is not an exact lot multiple.; Only 18 secondary observations are available; 19 October shows five trades and ₹437,304 turnover, without complete 20/60-day depth/restriction evidence. Contrary: Missing sessions are not zero trades, and deliverables are not volume. L remains NS despite a verified board lot. [MARKET19, MASTER19, SHP26] |\\n\\n`score_boundary_tests.json` moves GOV and MOAT independently ±1. Each changes BQS's bound by ±0.20 and IAS's bound by ±0.05; NS and the holds remain. The full Decimal50 contributions, original weights, bounds, caps and unrounded results are in `scores.json`. The methodology is a research policy, not an empirically validated prediction model.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nRepono supplies onsite operations, maintenance, material handling, polymer packaging and liquid-terminal support, plus offsite warehousing and consulting/engineering. Much of the historical business operates **customers' assets**, with people, equipment and safety processes deployed at the customer's site. It should not be valued as though it already owns a large network of petroleum terminals. FY26 parent operating revenue comprises **₹56.7168 crore O&M, ₹6.45 crore domestic consulting and ₹2.10 crore net export consulting**, totaling ₹65.2668 crore. The export figure is after a ₹1.17 crore reversal relating to an earlier cancelled contract. Gross profit is not presented as a comparable manufacturing measure. [AR26, notes21–26][AR26]\\n\\nThe cost base is substantial: FY26 employee expense ₹25.1895 crore, direct service costs ₹23.7960 crore, equipment rent ₹6.8763 crore within those direct costs, and warehouse rent ₹2.4403 crore in other expenses. These categories must not be added twice. Consulting can improve the mix, but has demonstrated collection risk. April 2025 disclosed headcount was 539, including 517 personnel deployed in O&M; safety, wages, staffing continuity and renewal economics matter more than a simple warehouse-area multiple. [AR26] [IPO, business/employees][IPO]\\n\\nManagement says contracts usually run three to five years, billing is often linked to throughput, and minimum guarantees can protect 80–95% of contract amounts. These are **M**, not independently inspected take-or-pay contracts. PSU work is retendered; management admits some losses and a low L1 strike rate. Revenue is earned before collection while payroll/vendor costs may fall due first. That timing is central to the cash model. [CALL25, pp5–6,10,14][CALL25]\\n\\nOffsite warehouses at Bhiwandi and Kutch/Mundra are leased. Offer-document leases extend to August 2027 and October 2029 respectively; renewal cost and relocation exposure are not zero merely because the model is called asset-light. Existing domestic operations, proposed Saudi O&M and the Mathura terminal have different economics and are kept separate. [IPO, properties][IPO]\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nThe plausible moat is technical qualification, operating experience in hazardous environments, trained teams and disruption costs when changing contractors. The evidence supports a useful niche, **not demonstrated pricing power**. The FY25 largest customer represented **34.37%**, the top five **82.45%**, and top ten **96.14%** of revenue. Supplier concentration was also high: top five **91.80%**. Customer credit quality does not protect against retender loss, delayed acceptance, disputed services or a cancelled export contract. The FY24 customer concentration table contains an amount/percentage inconsistency, so its printed total is not silently used as an exact comparative. [IPO, concentration tables][IPO]\\n\\n| Economically relevant comparator | Useful comparison | Why its multiple is not transferred |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Aegis Logistics | Established liquid/gas terminal network, connections and large-scale capex. January 2026 disclosures quantify capacity and project budgets, unlike Repono's unresolved new-terminal budget. | Owned assets, LPG sourcing/distribution, scale, portfolio and consolidated claims differ. Its success does not prove Repono's terminal returns. [AEGIS] |\\n| Aegis Vopak Terminals | Specialist tank-storage infrastructure and port access provide a stronger asset/barrier benchmark. | Asset-owner economics, capital intensity and Aegis/Vopak sponsorship differ; it overlaps with Aegis, so not an independent second valuation vote. [AVTL] |\\n| Mahindra Logistics | Contract logistics illustrates people/process execution, leased capacity and the importance of utilization. FY26 returned to PAT profitability after two loss years, showing that scale alone is not a moat. | Express, mobility and diversified logistics exposures differ; reported lease/claim conventions require adjustment. [MLL] |\\n| TVS Supply Chain Solutions | Integrated supply-chain and in-plant logistics is closer to the outsourced-service model; useful for client integration and technology comparisons. | Global network/transport mix, acquisitions, leases and financial scope differ. No unsupported current peer multiple is supplied. [TVS] |\\n\\nThese are business-model comparators, not a fabricated relative-valuation table. Superior infrastructure barriers and weaker/turnaround logistics economics are both represented. Repono's accessible opportunity is additional qualified contracts and customer sites, not the whole logistics or petroleum-storage TAM. A safe operating record, repeat renewal rates, realized escalation clauses and contract-level returns would strengthen MOAT; none is inferred from prominent customer logos.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\nThe measurement window was chosen **before scoring**: original standalone FY21 revenue ₹20.0357211 crore to FY26 standalone ₹65.2668 crore, five actual intervals, CAGR **26.64%**. This yields REV's starting score 8, no trajectory adjustment, capped at 8 for Medium confidence. The three-year FY23 trough-to-FY26 CAGR is **71.17%** and is shown, not chosen because it is larger. A ten-year CAGR is ND because eleven annual observations are unavailable for this 2017-incorporated issuer. [AR21] [AR22] [AR23] [AR26]\\n\\n| Period | Parent operating revenue | Change / interpretation |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| FY19 comparative | 3.2119785 | Early small base; not a mature-cycle anchor. |\\n| FY20 | 13.1965046 | Original statutory accounts. |\\n| FY21 | 20.0357211 | Five-year CAGR start. |\\n| FY22 | 15.8384182 | Contraction, not uninterrupted compounding. |\\n| FY23 | 13.0138330 | Another contraction; trough exaggerates subsequent CAGR. |\\n| FY24 | 34.0058580 | Addition ₹20.992025 crore; FY23 consolidated restatement versus FY24 standalone is not a strictly organic consolidated bridge. |\\n| FY25 | 51.1154640 | Addition ₹17.109606 crore, +50.31%. |\\n| FY26 | 65.2668000 | Addition ₹14.151336 crore, +27.69%. New terminal subsidiary revenue zero. |\\n\\nOriginal FY20–FY24 revenue is preserved separately from restated earnings/cash vintages. Former subsidiaries were divested in FY24; growth of the continuing parent is identifiable, but **price, throughput, new-site, consulting mix and pass-through costs are not fully decomposed**. The new terminal did not manufacture FY26 consolidated revenue growth. Historical former-subsidiary disposal, acquisitions, organic volume and headline percentage growth must not be conflated. [AR24] [IPO] [AR26]\\n\\nH1 FY26 operating revenue was **₹30.4889 crore**, +37.85% on ₹22.1173 crore in H1 FY25; management's ₹30.72 crore referred to **total income**, not operating revenue. H1 PAT was ₹3.2268 crore. FY26 less H1 gives H2 revenue ₹34.7779 crore and PAT ₹3.3546 crore. This is a same-year decomposition, not a new TTM. No H1 FY27 result was located by the cutoff; do not incorporate later November results or annualize one half-year mechanically. [HY25] [AR26]\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nGrowth access is credible but not free. The **₹9.6 crore, three-year Deepak Phenolics contract** has an average annual equivalent of ₹3.2 crore, roughly 4.9% of FY26 parent revenue. It is not automatically wholly incremental to existing revenue. Management's **₹240 crore backlog at 27 November 2025** covered the remaining execution of existing contracts over approximately 35–36 months, not ₹240 crore of newly won business. A ₹60–70 crore first-year execution indication is more informative than dividing backlog into an assumed windfall. Opening backlog, additions, execution, cancellations and margins are not reconciled by cutoff. [DEEPAK] [CALL25, pp3–4][CALL25]\\n\\nA bottom-up scaling check: on the model's analytical base entry run-rate of ₹77.014824 crore, 12% annual growth requires approximately ₹9.24 crore first-year additional revenue, almost three Deepak-sized annual equivalents. The bull entry run-rate is ₹81.5835 crore and 20% growth requires ₹16.32 crore of first-year additions, about five such equivalents. These comparisons test scale; they are not a forecast of five identical contracts. They require staffing, customer qualification, vendor mobilization and working capital, with no verified capacity/utilization schedule that can deliver them automatically. [A; model_inputs.json]\\n\\n**Project status ladder:**\\n\\n- Existing O&M and consulting: operating and revenue-generating, with disclosed concentration and collection risk.\\n- IPO equipment/software: partly acquired; substantial allocations remained unspent at 31 March 2026. Packing system allocation was unspent. Cost saving from replacing rentals remains a management thesis until realized net of depreciation, maintenance and financing. [AR26] [CALL25]\\n- Repono Terminal: acquired/controlled in November 2025; reported FY26 turnover zero; parent loan ₹3.0995 crore, group CWIP ₹3.7890 crore. Not mature terminal earnings. [AR26]\\n- Mathura step-down: incorporated 8 April 2026 with ₹1 lakh share subscription; Reliance arrangement announced 30 May. Cost, capacity, tariff, committed debt/equity, guarantees and complete parent recourse are not quantified in the filing. [MATHURA26] [RIL26]\\n- Railway ESP approval: a 30 September company-issued syndicated announcement describes a 20-year RIL arrangement and a target of operations within 36 months **of EPC commencement**, with formalities still needed before construction. The BSE attachment itself was inaccessible. This is M, not an independently read regulatory approval or proof of commissioning. [RAIL26]\\n- Saudi Repono GW: 51% interest announced in February 2026, formed January 2026; annual wording/perimeter is inconsistent. No underwritten revenue or terminal value is assigned. A separate ₹0.1362 crore formation advance in the parent notes concerns **UAE**, not automatically Saudi. [SAUDI26] [AR26]\\n\\nThe November 2025 call explicitly anticipated funding the terminal with **cash accruals, new debt and probably equity**. That is a financing warning, not a commitment from lenders or investors. Its contemporaneous explanation of other noncurrent assets as equipment advances is management commentary, not a reconciliation of FY26 FD/CFO classifications. [CALL25, pp6–8][CALL25]\\n\\nThe model charges finite IPO spending and ₹3–6 crore parent project contributions over the first three investment years, with **zero new-terminal/Saudi value**. Those contributions are bounded analyst stresses, **not** estimates of total project capex or maximum legal exposure. A truly large project could require much more. CAP remains NS despite computable aggregate proxies; CASH=3 imposes the GQS cap, while the missing CAP input keeps GQS NS.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\nPromoters held **72.47%** at 31 March 2026, with no pledge, non-disposal undertaking or other encumbrance reported then. The two main founders' combined holding is not the whole promoter-group percentage. Director remuneration was ₹1.20 crore, about 18.2% of parent PAT; related rent and related loans are disclosed and warrant commercial scrutiny, not an automatic allegation. Loans to Repono Green remain material after disposal of the ownership stake. No new warrant/ESOP/convertible claim was identified in the reviewed records; that is not a guarantee of absence after the cutoff. [SHP26] [AR26] [IPO]\\n\\n| Dated statement / commitment | Outcome by cutoff | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| November 2025: FY26 revenue around ₹65–66 crore | Operating revenue ₹65.2668 crore; total income ₹66.4532 crore | Broad revenue delivery, but definitions matter. [CALL25] [AR26] |\\n| November 2025: large terminal award expected by December / 30–45 days | RIL arrangement announced May 2026 | Milestone achieved later; not yet an operating-return record. [CALL25] [RIL26] |\\n| IPO objects planned in FY26 | ₹16.6535 crore earmarked balance at March 2026, including all packing allocation | Deployment delayed; disclosed ability to use subsequent years is not proof of diversion or of execution. [AR26] [IPO] |\\n| November 2025: leased MHE/forklift purchases should reduce costs | Equipment acquired, but equipment rent still ₹6.8763 crore and large pallet/furniture additions | Require like-for-like rental savings after maintenance/depreciation; no automatic margin uplift. [CALL25] [AR26] |\\n| November 2025: first Saudi business hoped for by March 2026 | Incorporation disclosed; audited revenue/perimeter not reconciled | Incorporation is not commercial delivery. [CALL25] [SAUDI26] [AR26] |\\n\\n**Full audit review:** Original FY20–FY23 main reports, CARO and IFC annexures were read; opinions were unmodified and IFC positive. FY24 main/CARO was read, with IFC **exempt**, not an omitted favorable opinion. Its KAM covers divestments of Repono Green 99.90% and Enerjaa 50% at face value above valuation. The previous auditor resigned on 15 April 2024 and V S Bapna & Associates was appointed. Do not claim an unchanged auditor history; a disclosed resignation alone proves no misconduct. Original FY25 annual audit was not recovered, so the offer restatement and FY26 comparative do **not** substitute for a falsely claimed full FY25 CARO/IFC review. FY26 complete standalone and consolidated main/CARO/IFC were read. `audit_evidence.json` records the coverage and exceptions. [AR20] [AR21] [AR22] [AR23] [AR24] [IPO] [AR26]\\n\\nFY26 audits are unmodified with positive IFC/audit-trail statements and no reported default, willful defaulter, fraud or one-year material uncertainty. The export receivable KAM, negative NCI, cash-flow classifications and proceeds mapping nevertheless require substantive reconciliation. Smaller CARO template contradictions, including the audit-committee wording, are retained proportionately. **G1 Conditional, not Fail; G2 Hold, not a fraud finding.**\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\n### Historical statements and restatement boundaries\\n\\n| Restated/current vintage | Revenue | PAT | Reported CFO | Cash capex | Gross debt | Cash equivalents | Equity | Scope |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| FY23 | 13.0138 | 0.5201 | -1.0319 | 1.5895 | 1.5250 | 0.2834 | 4.8658 | Consolidated former subsidiaries |\\n| FY24 | 34.0059 | 4.1822 | -0.0262 | 2.0693 | 3.5380 | 0.0507 | 9.0694 | Standalone |\\n| FY25 | 51.1155 | 5.1490 | 0.3296 | 1.4692 | 6.1325 | 0.0584 | 14.2185 | Standalone |\\n| FY26 | 65.2668 | 6.5814 | -18.2300 | 8.8755 | 9.1252 | 0.0337 | 44.5955 | Standalone |\\n\\nFY23 above is consolidated with former subsidiaries, FY24–FY25 are offer-document standalone restatements, and FY26 is current standalone statutory. Do not sum this entire series and call it a single unchanged consolidation perimeter. FY26 consolidated revenue ₹65.2668 crore, total PAT ₹6.5782 crore, owner PAT ₹6.5783 crore, CFO **−₹15.1323 crore**, cash capex ₹11.8892 crore, cash ₹0.1262 crore and gross debt ₹9.1342 crore are separately retained. Consolidated reported owner equity ₹45.1463 crore and NCI **−₹0.5595 crore** require reconciliation, not an automatic valuation benefit. [IPO, F7–F9][IPO] [AR26]\\n\\n| Original standalone vintage | Revenue | PAT | CFO | Cash capex | Cash equivalents | Equity |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY20 | 13.1965 | 1.2168 | 0.3531 | 0.0987 | 0.3587 | 1.2979 |\\n| FY21 | 20.0357 | 1.6178 | 4.4409 | 2.8307 | 0.1866 | 2.9157 |\\n| FY22 | 15.8384 | 1.4536 | 0.1621 | 0.4313 | 2.4651 | 4.3693 |\\n| FY23 | 13.0138 | 0.6242 | -1.0428 | 1.4095 | 0.2597 | 4.9935 |\\n| FY24 | 34.0059 | 4.0342 | 0.4359 | 2.2493 | 0.0507 | 9.0277 |\\n\\nOriginal statements use rupees in FY20–FY22 and thousands in FY23–FY24. Conversion is explicit in the inputs. FY24 original PAT ₹4.034215 crore becomes restated ₹4.182163 crore through gratuity +₹0.078989 crore and deferred tax +₹0.068959 crore. FY25 book PAT ₹5.100374 crore becomes restated ₹5.149041 crore through deferred tax +₹0.048667 crore. FY23 original standalone PAT and restated consolidated/owner PAT differ by both perimeter and adjustments. These are retained, not smoothed away. [AR20] [AR21] [AR22] [AR23] [AR24] [IPO, F21/F27][IPO]\\n\\nA further historical classification bridge is explicit: original FY23 CFO −₹1.042773 crore becomes −₹1.375684 crore in FY24's comparative, offset exactly by +₹0.325 crore financing and +₹0.007911 crore investing interest. Debt/AP shift by ₹0.325 crore; closing cash is unchanged. Original FY24 CFO is ₹0.435858 crore, verified from the page image rather than an OCR misread. [AR23] [AR24]\\n\\nFY26's FY25 comparative reports PAT ₹5.1004 crore, CFO **−₹1.3705 crore**, capex ₹1.1195 crore and equity ₹14.1281 crore, rather than the offer-restated figures. The CFO difference of **₹1.70011 crore** is largely offset by investing **₹1.698971 crore** and financing **₹0.001127 crore** in the opposite direction. FD/current investment ₹0.997855 crore, other noncurrent ₹0.351419 crore and PPE ₹0.349678 crore largely explain the investing movement. **Closing cash reconciles; cash is not alleged missing.** Detailed classifications and restatement carry-forward are still insufficiently explained for G2 clearance. [IPO, F9][IPO] [AR26, p72][AR26]\\n\\n### Earnings to cash, without a false capex excuse\\n\\nFY26 parent operating EBIT = PBT ₹9.1715 + finance ₹0.9820 − other income ₹1.1864 = **₹8.9671 crore**. Add depreciation ₹0.9976 for operating EBITDA **₹9.9647 crore**, or 15.27% of operating revenue. An EBITDA measure including other income is ₹11.1511 crore; it is not the same operating denominator. The ₹1.17 crore cancelled-contract export reversal is not an expense that can simply be added back. No normalized PAT claim is made beyond the transparent operating-earnings bridge. [AR26]\\n\\nReported FY26 parent CFO is **−₹18.2300 crore**. The source's rounded component lines sum to −₹18.2299 crore, a ₹0.0001 crore rounding difference. Important uses include receivables ₹3.6360 crore, short loans ₹0.4639 crore, subsidiary loan ₹3.0995 crore, other current assets ₹2.5204 crore and other noncurrent assets ₹17.1141 crore. Cash capex is in **investing**, not a reason by itself for weak CFO. The noncurrent-asset movement includes FD increases, deposit release and unamortized expenditure movements. [AR26, p72 and notes14/18/19][AR26]\\n\\n| Clearly labeled diagnostic | ₹ crore | What it does **not** establish |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| Reported CFO | −18.2300 | Not all operating deterioration. |\\n| Add exact FD increase 19.6766−1.3768 | +18.2998 | FDs are not automatically available cash. |\\n| CFO after FD reclassification only | +0.0698 | Not a complete clean operating CFO. |\\n| Also remove separately identified parent-to-subsidiary loan | +3.0995 | Group financing still consumes shareholder capital. |\\n| FD/subsidiary-loan adjusted diagnostic | +3.1693 | Other loans, IPO/tax advances and noncash issuance-asset movements remain. |\\n| Less cash finance cost classified in financing | −0.9820 | Diagnostic after financing charge = ₹2.1873 crore, not owner FCF. |\\n\\nSecurity deposits declined **₹0.2444 crore**; miscellaneous expenditure not written off declined **₹0.9413 crore**. The latter may reflect issue-cost treatment, not a cash receipt. H1 note6 capitalized IPO expenses ₹2.5135108 crore, and FY26 note3 deducts ₹2.7943 crore from securities premium; no specific reconciliation establishes that the ₹0.9413 crore movement is cash. Therefore the ₹3.1693 crore diagnostic is **not called normalized CFO**. [HY25] [AR26]\\n\\nPre-IPO FY24–FY25 restated parent cumulative CFO **₹0.303426 crore / PAT ₹9.331204 crore = 3.25%**. FY20–FY24 original parent cumulative conversion is 48.61%; FY21's strong CFO contains a ₹1.6721623 crore increase in other long-term liabilities that reverses in FY22. FY22 CFO is only ₹0.1620913 crore, visually checked, while a ₹1.8 crore investment withdrawal helps cash. FY24–FY26 reported cumulative conversion is −112.66%, but that is heavily distorted by FY26 FD/loan classification and is not the sole basis for CASH=3. [AR20] [AR21] [AR22] [AR23] [AR24] [IPO] [AR26]\\n\\nReceivables rose ₹11.7467→₹15.3827 crore. Balances over six months rose **₹0.2306→₹2.5422 crore**; the FY26 ageing schedule includes ₹2.0387 crore at six–twelve months, ₹0.4354 crore at one–two years and smaller older balances. Average-receivable days on total-revenue proxy rise from about 65.73 in FY25 to 75.86 in FY26. Closing-balance days are not confused with this average. Inventory and payable days have incompatible service-cost denominators; the model's inventory/revenue and 38%-of-sales payable-cost proxies are labeled assumptions, not audited cash-conversion-cycle statistics. [AR26] [IPO]\\n\\n### Capital returns and funding bridge\\n\\n| Measure | FY24 | FY25 | FY26 | Limitation |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| Operating EBITDA, excluding other income | 6.0432 | 8.1331 | 9.9647 | Restated/current vintage mix. |\\n| Standard-tax NOPAT, 27.82% assumption | 4.1455 | 5.2817 | 6.4725 | Analytical tax normalization, not cash tax. |\\n| Core trade-plus-fixed IC | 10.9558 | 17.4181 | 28.1437 | AR+inventory−AP+PPE+intangibles+CWIP; not full ONWC. |\\n| NOPAT / average core IC | 48.58% | 37.23% | 28.41% | FY24 opening perimeter differs; proxy, not full ROIC. |\\n| Operating EBIT / average equity plus gross debt (ROCE) | 60.46% | 44.40% | 24.21% | Defined financing-denominator ratio; retains idle IPO cash, unlike core operating IC. |\\n| Incremental NOPAT / incremental core IC | 74.28% | 17.58% | 11.10% | FY24 trough/perimeter distortion; FY26 projects have not matured. |\\n| Reported CFO less cash capex | −2.0955 | −1.1396 | −27.1055 | FD/loan classification matters; cash interest still separate. |\\n\\nFY25–FY26 incremental numerator is **₹1.1907152 crore NOPAT**, denominator **₹10.725603 crore core IC**, giving 11.10%, not evidence of attractive returns on all new capital. FY26 selected operating IC adds signed other operating assets/liabilities **−₹1.0194 crore**, reaching ₹27.1243 crore. Components are supplier advances, prepaids, employee advances, security deposits and GST input, less salaries/vendor/statutory employee payables, gratuity and expense provisions. The financing-side bridge is ₹27.4317 crore after excluding cash, FDs, nonoperating advances and the subsidiary equity investment, leaving an explicit **₹0.3074 crore unresolved tax/other classification residual**. It is not forced to zero. Full historical cohort economics and project cost are missing; CAP remains NS. [AR26, notes6–19][AR26]\\n\\nThe major asset addition deserves scrutiny: ₹5.35 crore wooden pallets were purchased in Maharashtra and transferred to Gujarat; furniture/fixture additions were ₹5.9402 crore. Auditor notes cite management's documentation representation, not an independent future-useful-life guarantee. New-year depreciation is not an appropriate automatic maintenance-capex estimate. The forward model tests maintenance at 1–4% of revenue. [AR26, PPE schedule][AR26]\\n\\n**Cash and debt cannot be netted indiscriminately.** Parent cash equivalents are ₹0.0337 crore, debt ₹9.1252 crore, including ICICI CC ₹8.4961 crore and vehicle debt ₹0.6291 crore. FDs total ₹19.6766 crore, of which ₹16.6535 crore is earmarked IPO and ₹3.0231 crore has no verified free-cash/lien map. Cash finance cost ₹0.9820 crore comprises interest ₹0.5446 crore, processing ₹0.3728 crore and bank-guarantee charges ₹0.0646 crore. Current coverage of EBIT/total finance cost is about 9.13×; favorable accounting coverage does not prove renewal liquidity. Bank guarantees ₹4.8425 crore and GST dispute ₹0.2460 crore are contingencies, not both cash spent or both zero risk. [AR26]\\n\\nIPO gross proceeds were **₹26.68032 crore**, net cash equity proceeds **₹23.8861 crore** after issue expenses. CARO's object table shows spent ₹10.02682 crore and remaining ₹16.6535 crore: equipment ₹4.7730, packing ₹1.6048, software ₹0.6962, working capital ₹9.5795. GCP allocation ₹6.67 crore is shown spent. These gross-object allocations and the separate issue-cost deduction need a gross/net reconciliation; do not count gross proceeds and net proceeds as two sources. The exchange proceeds form also contains a malformed amount/units presentation and incomplete object detail. These are reconciliation questions, not a proven use-of-proceeds violation. [AR26, CARO][AR26] [PROCEEDS26]\\n\\nThe rounded statutory cash bridge is: opening ₹0.0584 − CFO ₹18.2300 − capex ₹8.8755 − subsidiary equity ₹0.0010 + interest received ₹1.0687 + rent ₹0.1175 + net issue ₹23.8861 − term debt ₹0.4636 + short debt ₹3.4552 − financing cash ₹0.9820 ≈ closing **₹0.0337 crore**, with ₹0.0001 crore rounding residual. There is no dividend double count. Bonuses in FY21/FY23/FY25 are not economic dilution; the 2,779,200 new IPO shares increased pre-IPO 7,500,000 shares by 37.056%. Reported FY26 weighted-average EPS is ₹7.06 on 9,319,804.93 weighted shares. Current-share FY26 parent EPS is **₹6.4026**, versus restated FY25 ₹6.8654 on the post-bonus 7.5 million base, a decline of about 6.74%, despite total PAT growth. [AR26] [IPO]\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n### Cross-check, not a price target\\n\\nAt the conditional ₹73.90 reference, current-share parent P/E is **11.54×**, parent EV/operating EBITDA **8.54×**, EV/sales 1.30× and P/B 1.70×. EV = ₹75.963288 crore market value + ₹9.1252 crore debt − **only** ₹0.0337 crore cash = ₹85.054788 crore. No restricted FD or unrecovered related loan is subtracted; subsidiary/project value is not invented. Consolidated EV is NS because the NCI/perimeter bridge is unresolved. Reported FCF yield is deeply negative and classification-distorted; it is not a clean owner-earnings yield. These multiples are compatible with both undervaluation and a financing/value trap. [C from AR26/IPO and market reference; AR26] [IPO] [MARKET19]\\n\\n### Cash-first model and explicit assumptions\\n\\nThe model starts at **audited 31 March 2026**, not a fabricated October balance. Seven pre-entry periods total 202 days through 19 October; then 60 equal monthly periods and 12 actual continuation-model months. Every operating cycle pays cash costs before collection, with eight cycles per month by default. A ₹0.02 crore operating floor is checked after **every event**. There is no return after the first breach, even if later projected earnings would be positive. Failure in this counterfactual model is **not a claim the issuer actually defaulted in April 2026**. The missing actual bank bridge is precisely why R is NS.\\n\\n| A: assumption | Bear | Base | Bull |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| Pre-entry annual run-rate uplift over FY26 | 8% | 18% | 25% |\\n| Five-year annual revenue growth | −3% | 12% | 20% |\\n| Operating EBITDA margin | 8% | 14.5% | 17% |\\n| Receivable-days target | 120 | 90 | 75 |\\n| Inventory / sales days proxy | 3 | 2 | 2 |\\n| Payable days on 38%-of-sales cost proxy | 18 | 28 | 35 |\\n| Other signed operating capital / sales | +1.5% | −1.0% | −1.5% |\\n| Parent project contributions, first three years | ₹6 crore | ₹3 crore | ₹3 crore |\\n\\nShared assumptions: tax 27.82%; depreciation 1.53% of sales; maintenance capex 2% of sales; growth fixed-capital investment 15% of incremental annual revenue; other working balances converge over months rather than jumping for free; vehicle principal ₹0.1986 crore per year; CC ₹8.4961 crore repaid in investment month five with **no automatic rollover**. That date is a stress assumption, not a disclosed maturity. Existing debt cash charge 10%; separately optional new debt costs 14%, capped ₹2 crore and fully repaid at year five. Optional debt upfront fee is assumed **zero**, so gross draw equals net cash; this is unverified and optimistic, unlike the separately charged 3% equity issue cost. No dividend is paid and then also retained in equity value.\\n\\n**Funding modes:**\\n\\n1. **Strict verified-only:** no FD release, new equity or new debt before/through entry. All three cases breach the floor at the first modeled operating payment; no ordinary return. This demonstrates the need for a reconciled treasury bridge, not an actual insolvency conclusion.\\n2. **Purpose-matched conditional release:** existing IPO equipment/packing/software pools may be released only against matching cash capex; WC pool only against eligible operating payment shortfalls. Pools are finite. No tax, debt repayment or unrelated project is funded directly from an earmark. Availability, liens and release timing remain **unverified**, so this is not committed finance.\\n3. **Optional rescue after a successful pre-entry bridge only:** whole-share equity at a 25% discount to each modeled entry price, 3% issue costs, capped at the lower of authorized headroom **1,220,800 shares** and ₹7 crore net proceeds. Authorization headroom is not board/shareholder/regulatory approval or investor willingness. The discounted price is a stress input, not a conclusion about legally permissible issue pricing. A separate ₹2 crore debt sensitivity is not assumed bank sanction. At ₹73.90, maximum net equity is ₹6.56329548 crore and final shares 11,500,000. No rescue is allowed to repair a pre-entry breach retroactively.\\n\\n| Scenario | FD policy | Optional new shares | Optional new debt, ₹ crore | Outcome / five-year CAGR if earned |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| bear | purpose_matched | 0 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 7.8637 |\\n| bear | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 2.3799 |\\n| bear | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 2 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 0.4641 |\\n| base | purpose_matched | 0 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 7.0753 |\\n| base | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 0.5120 |\\n| base | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 2 | 4.79% |\\n| bull | purpose_matched | 0 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 3.5795 |\\n| bull | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 0 | 17.86% |\\n| bull | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 2 | 17.74% |\\n\\nMinimum whole-share survival searches retain every trial and the one-share-lower failure. Survival includes year-six funding, not merely positive year-five cash.\\n\\n| Scenario | Optional debt | Minimum new whole shares | Result | Conditional CAGR |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|---:|\\n| bear | 0 | No feasible rescue | NO_FEASIBLE_RESCUE_WITHIN_CAP | NS |\\n| bear | 2 | No feasible rescue | NO_FEASIBLE_RESCUE_WITHIN_CAP | NS |\\n| base | 0 | No feasible rescue | NO_FEASIBLE_RESCUE_WITHIN_CAP | NS |\\n| base | 2 | 959693 | CONDITIONAL_MINIMUM_WHOLE_SHARE_RESCUE | 5.14% |\\n| bull | 0 | 665808 | CONDITIONAL_MINIMUM_WHOLE_SHARE_RESCUE | 18.77% |\\n| bull | 2 | 309464 | CONDITIONAL_MINIMUM_WHOLE_SHARE_RESCUE | 19.19% |\\n\\nMore equity can lower per-share returns even when it improves cash survival. No case is called attractive solely because optional rescue makes an equation solvent.\\n\\n### Annual linked cash paths\\n\\nBelow are annual totals for the maximum bounded equity plus ₹2 crore optional-debt cases, under the conditional FD policy. Infeasible cases stop at their breach; missing years are **not zero or reconstructed ordinary returns**. Every monthly balance, partial failed period, operating collection/payment, earmark, financing event and debt/share balance is retained in the machine-readable full paths.\\n\\n| Case | Year | Revenue | EBITDA | ΔONWC | CFO after cash finance | Capex | Project cash | CC repayment | Term principal | Optional debt repayment | Closing cash |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| base | 1 | 86.2566 | 12.5072 | 2.7027 | 6.2036 | 7.5754 | 1.0000 | 8.4961 | 0.1986 | 0.0000 | 2.9571 |\\n| base | 2 | 96.6074 | 14.0081 | 2.2597 | 8.0439 | 3.4848 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1986 | 0.0000 | 6.3177 |\\n| base | 3 | 108.2003 | 15.6890 | 2.4448 | 9.1349 | 3.9029 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1220 | 0.0000 | 10.4276 |\\n| base | 4 | 121.1843 | 17.5717 | 2.7285 | 10.2685 | 4.3713 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 16.3248 |\\n| base | 5 | 135.7264 | 19.6803 | 3.0549 | 11.5260 | 4.8958 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 2.0000 | 20.9550 |\\n| bull | 1 | 97.9002 | 16.6430 | 2.3686 | 9.5725 | 8.8696 | 1.0000 | 8.4961 | 0.1986 | 0.0000 | 7.5773 |\\n| bull | 2 | 117.4802 | 19.9716 | 3.0388 | 11.6581 | 5.2866 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1986 | 0.0000 | 12.7503 |\\n| bull | 3 | 140.9763 | 23.9660 | 3.6685 | 14.0250 | 6.3439 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1220 | 0.0000 | 19.3093 |\\n| bull | 4 | 169.1715 | 28.7592 | 4.4047 | 16.8716 | 7.6127 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 28.5682 |\\n| bull | 5 | 203.0059 | 34.5110 | 5.2859 | 20.2861 | 9.1353 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 2.0000 | 37.7190 |\\n\\n### Earned terminal economics and claim bridge\\n\\nOnly a path that funds all five years is allowed to attempt a continuation year. Year six assumes 3.5% growth, a 15% capital charge and an incremental-return ceiling of 15%. It pays actual operating capital and maintenance/growth capex, plus extra economic reinvestment if needed so growth is not free: required net reinvestment ≥ NOPAT×growth/15%. **No new financing or FD releases are permitted during that continuation year.** Terminal FCFF equals NOPAT minus actual net reinvestment, and EV = FCFF/(capital charge−growth). This is a conditional capitalization of parent earnings, not a liquidating value or a perpetual high-growth multiple.\\n\\nA cash reserve equal to the worst continuation-year intra-period drawdown plus the floor is retained. Only cash above that reserve at year five is added to EV; outstanding debt is deducted once, current diluted shares divide equity once, and earmarked FDs, related loans, UAE/Saudi/Mathura value are not added. Terminal operating value is 100% of the operating exit-value calculation, with no interim distributions: substantial duration/terminal sensitivity remains even after the funding test. Static hurdle-entry prices in terminal records hold financing constant and are labeled diagnostics; the reverse-price searches below **reprice the rescue and recompute the whole path**.\\n\\n### Diagnostic exit-multiple normalization\\n\\nThe intrinsic, reinvestment-funded FCFF calculation remains primary. The following **8× / 10× / 12× year-five operating EBITDA** checks hold each already-surviving cash path fixed, retain exactly the same required operating reserve, add only the same excess year-five cash, deduct outstanding debt once and divide by actual diluted shares. This is a valuation-normalization diagnostic, not a preferred rerating assumption, a new funding source or an earned intrinsic price target. No failed path receives an exit price or return: all failed cases remain null in `multiple_normalization.json`. Parent operating EBITDA excludes other income, as in the historical analysis. All rows below require the maximum 1,220,800-share optional issue; optional debt is shown separately. Cash/earnings amounts are ₹ crore and exit prices are ₹ per share.\\n\\n| Conditional case | Optional new debt | Exit EV/EBITDA | Year-five EBITDA | Required reserve | Excess cash added | Debt deducted | Diluted shares | Diagnostic exit price | Diagnostic five-year CAGR |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| base | 2 | 8 | 19.6803 | 1.2665 | 19.6885 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 154.03 | 15.82% |\\n| base | 2 | 10 | 19.6803 | 1.2665 | 19.6885 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 188.25 | 20.56% |\\n| base | 2 | 12 | 19.6803 | 1.2665 | 19.6885 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 222.48 | 24.66% |\\n| bull | 0 | 8 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 36.9038 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 272.17 | 29.79% |\\n| bull | 0 | 10 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 36.9038 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 332.19 | 35.07% |\\n| bull | 0 | 12 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 36.9038 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 392.20 | 39.63% |\\n| bull | 2 | 8 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 35.8932 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 271.29 | 29.71% |\\n| bull | 2 | 10 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 35.8932 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 331.31 | 34.99% |\\n| bull | 2 | 12 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 35.8932 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 391.33 | 39.57% |\\n\\nA multiple does not pay maintenance capex, working capital or creditors. Attractive results in this table cannot override an intrinsic return shortfall, unavailable funding, the year-six cash proof, or unresolved gates. The machine-readable records retain the intrinsic implied EV/EBITDA alongside each diagnostic multiple for comparison.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations, funding boundaries and sensitivities\\n\\n| Conditional case / variable | Equity hurdle | Feasible root bracket |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| base price | 12.00% | No feasible bracket on grid |\\n| base price | 15.00% | No feasible bracket on grid |\\n| base price | 18.00% | No feasible bracket on grid |\\n| bull price | 12.00% | 97.034686–97.034688 |\\n| bull price | 15.00% | 83.862441–83.862443 |\\n| bull price | 18.00% | 73.065362–73.065364 |\\n| base growth | 15.00% | No feasible bracket on grid |\\n| base margin | 15.00% | 0.203076–0.203076 |\\n\\nEach reverse request retains all scan and bisection paths, including failed ones. Feasibility-transition boundaries are separately bisected; no root is interpolated across an insolvent interval. No feasible bracket means **none found on the declared domain**, not a global theorem. Base-case price scans cover ₹20–120, growth 0–50%, and margin 8–24%; bull price scans cover ₹20–150. The maximum whole-share issue is repriced at each trial price, subject to both caps. At the ₹73.90 reference and base growth/working-capital settings, the conditional 15% hurdle requires an operating EBITDA margin around **20.31%**, well above FY26's 15.27%, with optional funding still required. Raising growth alone can destroy feasibility before achieving the hurdle. In the declared base price domain, the first feasible boundary is about ₹58.09408 and the conditional return there is about 9.82%, below even the 12% hurdle. The base growth feasibility boundary is approximately 23.9294%, where return is about 12.50%; higher growth breaches cash before reaching 15%. These are local modeled boundaries, not global impossibility claims.\\n\\n| Two-way test: growth / margin, or terminal capital charge / growth | Conditional result |\\n|---|---:|\\n| growth_margin/.08/.10 | -9.78% |\\n| growth_margin/.08/.145 | 2.40% |\\n| growth_margin/.08/.17 | 6.97% |\\n| growth_margin/.08/.20 | 11.63% |\\n| growth_margin/.12/.10 | Cash breach; no return |\\n| growth_margin/.12/.145 | 4.79% |\\n| growth_margin/.12/.17 | 9.64% |\\n| growth_margin/.12/.20 | 14.54% |\\n| growth_margin/.20/.10 | Cash breach; no return |\\n| growth_margin/.20/.145 | 9.85% |\\n| growth_margin/.20/.17 | 15.18% |\\n| growth_margin/.20/.20 | 20.58% |\\n| growth_margin/.30/.10 | Cash breach; no return |\\n| growth_margin/.30/.145 | Cash breach; no return |\\n| growth_margin/.30/.17 | 22.50% |\\n| growth_margin/.30/.20 | 28.46% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.12/.02 | 22.69% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.12/.035 | 23.84% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.12/.05 | 25.31% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.15/.02 | 17.47% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.15/.035 | 17.74% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.15/.05 | 18.01% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.18/.02 | 13.65% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.18/.035 | 13.48% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.18/.05 | 13.19% |\\n\\nThe full one-way tests additionally vary receivable days 60–120, maintenance 1–4% of sales, CC repayment months 3/5/12/60, project contributions ₹0/3/6/12 crore, cash cycles 4/8/16/32, existing borrowing cost 8–14%, cash floor ₹0.01/0.02/0.05 crore and growth fixed-capital intensity 10/15/25%. Each recomputes cash, tax, capital, debt, dilution and terminal viability, not merely terminal P/E. ₹0 project cash is an optional-project exclusion sensitivity, not proof the contract costs nothing. Execution charges/impact are not estimable from the available tape; a 2% combined entry/exit friction would further reduce a five-year CAGR by roughly 0.4 percentage points, without repairing a funding failure. **No supported buy-price range is issued.**\\n\\n### Liquidity implementation\\n\\nThe official master verifies 1,200 shares per lot. Secondary 19 October data show five trades and ₹437,304 turnover; deliverable quantity 6,000 is **not total traded volume**. Only 18 observed dates are available, not a complete 20/60-session sample. The sample-only 5% participation estimate is 5.49 days for ₹5 lakh, with 2% and 10% alternatives in `liquidity.json`. Missing dates are not inserted as zero and this diagnostic does not earn an L score. Actual exit may be dominated by one-sided circuits, spread, impact and lot granularity. G4 remains Hold. [MASTER19] [MARKET19]\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\n**Impairment risk: High, with uncertain loss severity, not a quantified probability.** The decisive variable is whether customer collections and available, legally usable liquidity fund obligations before cash is due. The linked loss path is: a concentrated customer is lost or disputed → utilization and margins fall → aged receivables cannot fund payroll/vendor payments → CC is not renewed while project contributions continue → discounted equity is needed or unavailable → dilution, asset sales or claim impairment fall on existing shareholders. A large accounting FD balance does not interrupt this sequence if earmarked or pledged. [IPO] [AR26; A stress model]\\n\\nThe strongest contrary evidence to the cautious interpretation is real revenue growth, profitable current contracts, a low gross-debt/operating-EBITDA ratio of about 0.92×, unmodified audits and earmarked assets that may in practice be readily releasable. If bank evidence confirms that access and a credible terminal budget is fully financed, the strict case is too pessimistic. Conversely, promoter holding, brand-name customers, railway design approval and ₹240 crore management backlog do not by themselves resolve cash claims.\\n\\nThe bear cash path fails within the modeled horizon even at the bounded optional issue/debt assumptions. **An ordinary bear target and CAGR are withheld after breach**; neither a negative bank balance nor zero is used as a fake share price. Liquidation recovery is ND without enforceable collateral rank, guarantees, receivable recoveries and project obligations. A temporary quote decline differs from this permanent-loss mechanism; a lower quote alone does not repair G2/G3/G4.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\nA multibagger claim is **speculative / unsupported** at this stage. Five-times and ten-times share-price outcomes over five years require approximately 37.97% and 58.49% annual price growth. At unchanged net margin and P/E, a maximum modeled 11.88% increase in shares means revenue would need about 5.594× or 11.188× FY26, roughly **₹365 crore or ₹730 crore**. This arithmetic ignores the much harder problem of financing the capital, qualifying contracts, maintaining margins and protecting per-share claims. It is not a target or a success probability. A mature terminal multiple may differ from a service multiple, but no unsupported rerating is used to bridge that gap. [C from current shares/AR26; A unchanged economics]\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\nThis is a research cadence, not an automatically created reminder.\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Constructive evidence / milestone | Warning and thesis-break distinction | Review source / timing |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Export reversal and ageing | ₹1.17 crore reversal; >6-month AR ₹2.5422 crore | Cash receipts against identified old balances; reconciliation of cancelled contract | Warning: further ageing or reclassification; break: material recurring uncollectible revenue without credible recognition controls | Next results, ageing notes and auditor report |\\n| Treasury availability | Cash ₹0.0337 crore; earmarked FD ₹16.6535 crore | Bank-level maturities/liens and eligible object-release schedule | Warning: restricted cash used in headline net debt; break: evidenced payment default/unfinanceable essential operations | Facility and proceeds disclosures |\\n| CC renewal | ₹8.4961 crore CC | Signed sanction, maturity, covenant and undrawn-headroom terms | Warning: overdue renewal or increased collateral; break: withdrawal with no funded alternative | Bank/rating filings, annual notes |\\n| IPO objects and costs | Equipment4.7730, packing1.6048, software0.6962, WC9.5795 unspent | Objects-to-invoices-to-FD and gross/net issue-cost reconciliation | Warning: further unexplained delays; break: substantiated diversion, not delay alone | Half-year proceeds update |\\n| Mathura terminal | May agreement; September ESP announcement; no disclosed complete budget | Full capex, capacity, tariff, EPC start, committed financing and recourse | Warning: new parent guarantees before finance; break: binding unfunded obligation exceeding credible resources | Material disclosures before construction |\\n| Parent/subsidiary claims | Terminal100%, Saudi51% notice, negative NCI0.5595 | Auditor-backed consolidation and opening-equity reconciliation | Warning: further inconsistent ownership; break: material unreconciled misstatement or minority harm | Corrected accounts and subsidiary statements |\\n| Related loans | Green3.1357, Terminal3.0995 crore | Commercial terms, cash interest/principal collection, board approvals | Warning: growth without purpose/recovery; break: substantiated abusive allocation | RPT filings/notes each half-year |\\n| Contract economics | Top-five FY25 share82.45%; management backlog240 crore | Renewal/expiry schedule, executed backlog bridge, cash margin by cohort | Warning: price cuts or concentrated loss; break: sustained negative cash unit economics | Contract updates/results |\\n| Liquidity | Official lot1200; incomplete tape | Complete official20/60-series, restriction notices, executable two-way depth | Warning: one-sided circuits; break for new purchase: effectively untradeable position | Before any investment consideration |\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Research priority / wait for evidence. Hold — unresolved.** This is not “buy because P/E is low,” not “avoid because SME,” and not a governance accusation. BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS; the record belongs in **pending evidence**, outside numerical rankings. It does not meet the compounder criteria, and the full classification matrix is overridden by unresolved gates rather than applied to invented midpoint scores.\\n\\nThe next decisive documents are: (1) the consolidation/NCI and cash-classification reconciliation; (2) a restricted-versus-available bank and proceeds map with CC renewal terms; (3) a complete, committed terminal funding and parent-recourse package; and (4) verified market implementation data. Better collections and demonstrated incremental cash returns would then allow CAP/BAL/R/P/L to be reconsidered. A different quote changes valuation inputs, not historical BQS/GQS. No trade is executed or position recommendation implied.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n### Evidence and reconstruction ledger\\n\\n- **Primary audit/history:** original FY20–FY24 issuer annual reports, FY25 SEBI offer restatements and current FY26 report. Full applicable original audit annexures were reviewed; FY25 original remains a declared gap. Source dates, locators, hashes and per-claim limitations are in `source_register.json` and `audit_evidence.json`.\\n- **Current business/capital:** November 2025 call, September 2025 Deepak contract, February 2026 Saudi notice, April Mathura incorporation, May RIL arrangement, March shareholding and proceeds filings. Management forecasts remain M, not V outcomes.\\n- **Price/implementation:** official19October interop master for identity/shares/lot only; secondary dated market rows for the reference price and diagnostic turnover. No false official closing-price claim.\\n- **Analyst inputs:** every forecast and cash-timing assumption is declared in `model_inputs.json`; no terminal/Saudi revenue is hidden in the base forecast. No unsupported facility is treated as committed.\\n- **Canonical computation:** `build.py` and `model_engine.py` produce Decimal50 results, 325 unique full paths and 451 aliases, including all failed scenarios, reverse/root candidates and whole-share rescue trials. Deduplication is by full content hash, with no omitted events or decimal digits. `verify.py` independently checks event accounting, claims, caps, terminal reinvestment/reserve, scores and root/adjacent-share endpoints.\\n- **Recovery:** compact contains all authored minimum inputs and executable code; full also contains generated results. `python guarded_rebuild.py --clean` deletes generated outputs and prior attestations, forbids undeclared/external reads and network/process calls during rebuilding, then recreates and verifies them. Neither archive contains downloaded PDFs, OCR, third-party helper code, credentials or private file identifiers. Rebuild attestations demonstrate reproducibility, **not investment accuracy**.\\n\\nOpen questions are substantive, not empty placeholders: precise FY25 CFO reclassification and restatement carry-forward; NCI and foreign-subsidiary perimeter; the ₹0.9413 crore issuance-asset movement; IPO gross/net object treatment; FD liens and bank release terms; old receivable cash receipts; related-loan recoverability; project total budget, guarantees and funding; contracted minimum guarantees/termination rights; current ownership and tradability. Model conclusions remain conditional until these are answered.\\n\\n**Update log, cutoff 20 October 2026:** Initial v3.0 deep record. Corrected headline negative-CFO interpretation for FD classification; retained rather than erased contradictory fiscal vintages. Added full historical audit coverage, current official lot corroboration, whole-share optional funding and terminal cash proof. No prior score is silently carried forward. Retrieval/rebuild occurred after the cutoff, but no post-cutoff operating event is used.\\n\\n[AR20]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2019-2020.pdf\\n[AR21]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2020-2021.pdf\\n[AR22]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2021-2022.pdf\\n[AR23]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2022-2023.pdf\\n[AR24]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2023-2024.pdf\\n[AR26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/Annual_Report/Repono_Annual_Report_2026_Final.pdf\\n[IPO]: https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/aug-2025/1754566211166_910.pdf\\n[HY25]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Outcome_of_the_Board_Meeting_13Nov25.pdf\\n[CALL25]: https://repono.in/H1-FY26-Concall-Transcript-Repono%20Limited_27Nov25.pdf\\n[SHP26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/Share_Holding_Pattern/SHP_31Mar26.pdf\\n[DEEPAK]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Intimation_for_awarding_of_order_or_contract_11Sep25.pdf\\n[SAUDI26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Acquisition_24Feb26.pdf\\n[MATHURA26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Acquisition_08Apr26.pdf\\n[RIL26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Longterm_arrangement_agreement_with_Reliance_Industries_Ltd_30May26.pdf\\n[RAIL26]: https://companyvoice.in/repono-receives-railway-approval-for-engineering-scale-planesp-of-gati-shakti-cargo-petroleum-oil-terminal-at-ajhai-mathura/\\n[PROCEEDS26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/Statement_of_Derivatives_Variations/Statement_of_Derivatives_Variations_M26.pdf\\n[MASTER19]: https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz\\n[MARKET19]: https://www.indiainfoline.com/company/repono-ltd-historical-data\\n[AEGIS]: https://aegisindia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/ALL_SE_TranscriptEarningQ3_Signed.pdf\\n[AVTL]: https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/may-2025/1747907426089.pdf\\n[MLL]: https://www.mahindra.com/annual-report-FY2026/33/\\n[TVS]: https://www.tvsscs.com/investor-relations/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/TVS-Supply-Chain_Annual-Report_FY25_hd.pdf\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\n**Hold — unresolved; a specialist-services expansion and capital-allocation watchlist, not an evidence-backed compounder or an eligible new-purchase case.** Repono operates customers' petroleum, petrochemical and packaging facilities and provides warehousing and consulting. Parent revenue has grown at 26.64% over FY21–FY26, and specialist qualifications and multiyear contracts are useful strengths. But competition is price-led, cash realization was weak before the IPO, and expansion into owning/building a terminal changes the funding burden. The apparent bargain at the secondary ₹73.90 reference price cannot yet be underwritten: BQS, GQS and IAS are **NS**, with explicit coverage and bounds below. The FY26 negative CFO is **not all operating burn** because it includes large FD placements, but those deposits are not verified free cash either. Reconciled accounts, a bank/proceeds map, committed terminal funding and observable collections, rather than another customer announcement, would change this assessment. [AR26] [IPO] [CALL25]\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n| Item | Research basis |\\n|---|---|\\n| Exact issuer | Repono Limited, formerly Repono Private Limited; CIN L74999MH2017PLC290217; incorporated 6 February 2017; BSE SME listing 4 August 2025. |\\n| Security | ISIN INE15WN01014, BSE 544463. Official 19 October interop master independently corroborates issuer, 10,279,200 issued shares and 1,200-share board lot. It does not validate a trade price or current surveillance status. |\\n| Valuation reference | ₹73.90, IIFL dated 19 October close, corroborated by Trendlyne. Investing shows ₹74.10 against a delayed intraday timestamp; discrepancy retained. BSE bhavcopy not retrieved. Not an executable quote. |\\n| Equity value | ₹75.9633 crore at current issued shares; no inference that historical weighted-average shares equal today's claims. |\\n| Financial perimeter | Consolidated FY26 retained alongside standalone. Parent operations used for the cash/valuation model because new terminal subsidiary has no revenue and consolidated negative NCI / Saudi scope are unresolved. This is not a disguised consolidated fair value. |\\n| Accounting | Indian GAAP Accounting Standards **AS**, including AS25 interim reporting, not Ind AS. Rental costs remain operating expense; no fabricated Ind AS116 liability. |\\n| Horizon / hurdles | Five years after the analytical entry date; 15% nominal INR equity hurdle, before investor taxes. Terminal capital charge separately assumed 15%, tested at 12%/18%; it is not an estimated issuer WACC. |\\n| Implementation | Illustrative ₹5 lakh reference, not a suitable allocation recommendation. Five lots cost ₹443,400, six ₹532,080 before charges. |\\n\\nIdentity and lot are supported by [IPO] and [MASTER19]. Financial scope is from [AR26]; price limitations are from [MARKET19].\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Evidence and resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | Conditional | No substantiated serious integrity or minority violation located; related finance and treasury deployment require oversight. Reconcile related-party approvals, terms and recoveries; contrary serious evidence would change this gate. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Audits are unmodified, but FY25 cash-flow vintages, FY26 negative NCI / foreign-subsidiary perimeter and issuance-asset cash classifications are unresolved. Obtain auditor/issuer reconciliation of scope, equity/NCI, prior CFO classification and issuance assets, supported by bank/proceeds mapping. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | No reported default; FD availability, CC renewal and full terminal funding cannot be underwritten without assumed releases/rescue. Obtain facilities, maturities/covenants, FD liens, release calendar, committed financing and the complete project cash budget. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | Identity and lot verified, but complete official 20/60-day tape, restrictions, spread and depth are not verified for the reference position. Verify exchange tape and executable depth/restrictions for the lot-adjusted position; a last trade is not assured execution. |\\n\\n**Confidence by subject:** High on exact issuer/issued shares/lot and transcription of identified figures; Medium on historical parent operations, audits and governance assessment; Medium on underlying recent revenue expansion, but Low on a price/volume decomposition; Low on full incremental capital returns, consolidated claim reconciliation, available liquidity, terminal financing and investment valuation. **Overall investment confidence: Low**, the weakest material area, not an average. Unmodified opinions are positive evidence, not an assurance of fraud absence or future financing. Gross profit and manufacturing inventory days are NA as comparable service-business measures; separately identified contract assets and complete lease-commitment schedules are ND, not assumed zero.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\nThe actual Master Prompt v3.0 weights are used without renormalization. Each of the nine quality factors and four IAS factors is assigned once and reused. Unknowns are not assigned zero, five, a peer average or a capped point score.\\n\\n| Measure | Raw numeric score | Final | Coverage | Raw mathematical missing-data bound | Applicable cap | Post-cap bound |\\n|---|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| BQS | NS | NS | 80.00% | 4.360–6.160 | Not applied | 4.360–6.160 |\\n| GQS | NS | NS | 85.00% | 5.400–6.750 | 5 | 5.000–5.000 |\\n| IAS | NS | NS | 20.00% | 2.240–7.640 | Not applied | 2.240–7.640 |\\n\\nBQS known contribution = 0.20×5 + 0.20×5 + 0.12×8 + 0.10×5 + 0.10×3 + 0.08×5 = **4.16**, with CAP/BAL weight 0.20 missing. Hence 4.16 + [0.20, 2.00] = **4.36–6.16**, not a point estimate. GQS known contribution is **5.25**, missing CAP weight 0.15, giving **5.40–6.75** before the persistent-CASH≤3 cap of 5. Its capped bound collapses to 5–5, **but the score remains NS**. This does not prove a GQS of 5.\\n\\nIAS directly scoreable coverage is 20% (D only). Its BQS input is the **post-cap BQS bound**, weighted once, producing **2.24–7.64**, not an IAS estimate. Effective nested evidence coverage is 40%, disclosed separately rather than substituted for direct coverage. There is **no governance or funding numeric override** because G1/G3 have not failed on sufficient evidence. G2 and G4 holds independently prohibit a favorable investment classification. Persistent poor cash realization, not merely an IPO FD placement or a single capex year, supports the GQS cap.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence, contrary evidence and rationale |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | 5 | Medium | Current main and IFC opinions are unmodified; promoters held 72.47%, with no reported encumbrance at March 2026.; Financing to divested Repono Green remains ₹3.1357 crore; director pay is ₹1.2 crore and capital-allocation transparency needs improvement. Contrary: No substantiated diversion or minority abuse was found. Unresolved financial/proceeds inconsistencies create G2/G3 holds, not a fabricated fraud finding. [AR26, IPO, AR24, SHP26] |\\n| MOAT | 5 | Medium | Specialist hazardous-product handling, trained teams and multiyear contracts support qualification and switching friction.; Management describes L1 retendering, lost contracts and a low bid strike rate; FY25 largest customer was 34.37% and top five 82.45%. Contrary: Prestigious clients and qualification do not establish pricing power; public tenders reopen competition. [IPO, CALL25, DEEPAK] |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | Chosen parent FY21–FY26 revenue ₹20.0357211→₹65.2668 crore gives five-year CAGR in the 20–30% band.; FY25–FY26 revenue rose 27.69%; the new terminal subsidiary had zero revenue, so it did not create reported turnover growth. Contrary: FY21–FY23 contraction makes the three-year recovery rate misleading. Price/volume and customer cohorts are ND. Starting score 8, no trajectory adjustment, Medium-confidence ceiling 8. [AR21, AR22, AR23, AR24, IPO, AR26, HY25] |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | Existing O&M contracts usually run three to five years; management stated ₹240 crore remaining backlog over about 36 months in November 2025.; A ₹9.6 crore three-year Deepak contract is documented; terminal agreement and railway plan approval provide access, not operating cash returns. Contrary: Backlog is not independently reconciled and contractual guarantees/termination rights were not audited. Retenders, energy transition and project execution constrain duration. [CALL25, DEEPAK, RIL26, RAIL26] |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | Core trade-plus-fixed-capital incremental proxies can be calculated, but exclude some operating balances and do not establish project-cohort returns.; FY26 parent cash capex was ₹8.8755 crore and total loans/advances ₹6.689 crore; Mathura cost, funding, tariffs and returns remain ND. Contrary: Existing profitable asset-light services are a positive. Aggregate accounting returns cannot establish incremental terminal returns. NS, rather than a made-up weak or neutral score. [AR26, IPO, CALL25] |\\n| CASH | 3 | Medium | Pre-IPO FY24–FY25 restated CFO was ₹0.303426 crore versus PAT ₹9.331204 crore; original FY22–FY23 cash realization was also weak.; Receivables older than six months rose ₹0.2306→₹2.5422 crore, alongside the material ₹1.17 crore export credit-note reversal. Contrary: FY26 reported CFO includes FD placements and subsidiary finance. These are separately diagnosed, not labeled clean normalization. The growth cap reflects repeated weak realization, not capex or FD placement alone. [AR22, AR23, AR24, IPO, AR26, HY25] |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | Parent cash is ₹0.0337 crore versus debt ₹9.1252 crore; IPO FDs ₹16.6535 crore and other FDs ₹3.0231 crore lack a verified availability/lien map.; CC renewal, undrawn facilities and committed terminal finance are ND; bank guarantees ₹4.8425 crore and GST dispute ₹0.2460 crore add contingent exposure. Contrary: Debt/EBITDA is moderate and CARO reports no default. Evidence is insufficient for resilient funding or an evidenced unfinanceable default: NS and G3 Hold. [AR26, RIL26, CALL25] |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | November 2025 FY26 revenue indication of ₹65–66 crore compares with ₹65.2668 crore operating revenue achieved.; The terminal award anticipated by December 2025 was announced in May 2026; IPO equipment/packing/software allocations remained materially unspent in March. Contrary: New contracts and revenue were delivered. Saudi commercial revenue is not reconciled against the March 2026 aspiration. Mixed execution, not automatic failure. [CALL25, AR26, RIL26, SAUDI26] |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium | Operating EBIT grows, but other income ₹1.1864 crore contains interest and is not entirely service earnings.; FY26 current-share parent EPS ₹6.4026 is below restated FY25 ₹6.8654 on the pre-IPO, bonus-adjusted base; dilution offsets total-profit growth. Contrary: No arbitrary addback of the export reversal or all finance charges. IPO expense charged to premium is not operating profit. Total-profit growth is real, but per-share quality is mixed. [IPO, AR26, HY25] |\\n| R | NS | Low | Strict treasury modeling breaches the cash floor before entry; no ordinary return is reported after a breach.; Conditional base survival requires unverified FD access plus optional bounded equity and debt, so its calculated CAGR is not a supported base return. Contrary: Conditional bull success is not a base investment return. R remains NS regardless of a displayed conditional CAGR. [AR26, MODEL] |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | Aged receivables, the export reversal and customer concentration create a credible loss-of-collection path.; Linked operating, CC nonrenewal and project stresses overwhelm bounded equity; existing shareholders face dilution and uncertain recovery. Contrary: Profitable contracts and unmodified audits are positives. Material impairment risk is not a forecast default probability or a liquidation valuation. [AR26, IPO, MODEL] |\\n| P | NS | Low | Price, growth and margin roots are solved only along explicitly cash-feasible paths, with repriced rescue shares.; Project economics, FD access and official trade-price verification remain incomplete; conditional roots are not market-certified expectations. Contrary: Simple P/E looks modest but does not establish a margin of safety. No defensible expectation score follows while funding and claims remain unresolved. [MODEL, MARKET19, RIL26] |\\n| L | NS | Low | The official 19 October lot is 1,200 shares, worth ₹88,680 at the secondary price; ₹5 lakh is not an exact lot multiple.; Only 18 secondary observations are available; 19 October shows five trades and ₹437,304 turnover, without complete 20/60-day depth/restriction evidence. Contrary: Missing sessions are not zero trades, and deliverables are not volume. L remains NS despite a verified board lot. [MARKET19, MASTER19, SHP26] |\\n\\n`score_boundary_tests.json` moves GOV and MOAT independently ±1. Each changes BQS's bound by ±0.20 and IAS's bound by ±0.05; NS and the holds remain. The full Decimal50 contributions, original weights, bounds, caps and unrounded results are in `scores.json`. The methodology is a research policy, not an empirically validated prediction model.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nRepono supplies onsite operations, maintenance, material handling, polymer packaging and liquid-terminal support, plus offsite warehousing and consulting/engineering. Much of the historical business operates **customers' assets**, with people, equipment and safety processes deployed at the customer's site. It should not be valued as though it already owns a large network of petroleum terminals. FY26 parent operating revenue comprises **₹56.7168 crore O&M, ₹6.45 crore domestic consulting and ₹2.10 crore net export consulting**, totaling ₹65.2668 crore. The export figure is after a ₹1.17 crore reversal relating to an earlier cancelled contract. Gross profit is not presented as a comparable manufacturing measure. [AR26, notes21–26][AR26]\\n\\nThe cost base is substantial: FY26 employee expense ₹25.1895 crore, direct service costs ₹23.7960 crore, equipment rent ₹6.8763 crore within those direct costs, and warehouse rent ₹2.4403 crore in other expenses. These categories must not be added twice. Consulting can improve the mix, but has demonstrated collection risk. April 2025 disclosed headcount was 539, including 517 personnel deployed in O&M; safety, wages, staffing continuity and renewal economics matter more than a simple warehouse-area multiple. [AR26] [IPO, business/employees][IPO]\\n\\nManagement says contracts usually run three to five years, billing is often linked to throughput, and minimum guarantees can protect 80–95% of contract amounts. These are **M**, not independently inspected take-or-pay contracts. PSU work is retendered; management admits some losses and a low L1 strike rate. Revenue is earned before collection while payroll/vendor costs may fall due first. That timing is central to the cash model. [CALL25, pp5–6,10,14][CALL25]\\n\\nOffsite warehouses at Bhiwandi and Kutch/Mundra are leased. Offer-document leases extend to August 2027 and October 2029 respectively; renewal cost and relocation exposure are not zero merely because the model is called asset-light. Existing domestic operations, proposed Saudi O&M and the Mathura terminal have different economics and are kept separate. [IPO, properties][IPO]\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\nThe plausible moat is technical qualification, operating experience in hazardous environments, trained teams and disruption costs when changing contractors. The evidence supports a useful niche, **not demonstrated pricing power**. The FY25 largest customer represented **34.37%**, the top five **82.45%**, and top ten **96.14%** of revenue. Supplier concentration was also high: top five **91.80%**. Customer credit quality does not protect against retender loss, delayed acceptance, disputed services or a cancelled export contract. The FY24 customer concentration table contains an amount/percentage inconsistency, so its printed total is not silently used as an exact comparative. [IPO, concentration tables][IPO]\\n\\n| Economically relevant comparator | Useful comparison | Why its multiple is not transferred |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Aegis Logistics | Established liquid/gas terminal network, connections and large-scale capex. January 2026 disclosures quantify capacity and project budgets, unlike Repono's unresolved new-terminal budget. | Owned assets, LPG sourcing/distribution, scale, portfolio and consolidated claims differ. Its success does not prove Repono's terminal returns. [AEGIS] |\\n| Aegis Vopak Terminals | Specialist tank-storage infrastructure and port access provide a stronger asset/barrier benchmark. | Asset-owner economics, capital intensity and Aegis/Vopak sponsorship differ; it overlaps with Aegis, so not an independent second valuation vote. [AVTL] |\\n| Mahindra Logistics | Contract logistics illustrates people/process execution, leased capacity and the importance of utilization. FY26 returned to PAT profitability after two loss years, showing that scale alone is not a moat. | Express, mobility and diversified logistics exposures differ; reported lease/claim conventions require adjustment. [MLL] |\\n| TVS Supply Chain Solutions | Integrated supply-chain and in-plant logistics is closer to the outsourced-service model; useful for client integration and technology comparisons. | Global network/transport mix, acquisitions, leases and financial scope differ. No unsupported current peer multiple is supplied. [TVS] |\\n\\nThese are business-model comparators, not a fabricated relative-valuation table. Superior infrastructure barriers and weaker/turnaround logistics economics are both represented. Repono's accessible opportunity is additional qualified contracts and customer sites, not the whole logistics or petroleum-storage TAM. A safe operating record, repeat renewal rates, realized escalation clauses and contract-level returns would strengthen MOAT; none is inferred from prominent customer logos.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\nThe measurement window was chosen **before scoring**: original standalone FY21 revenue ₹20.0357211 crore to FY26 standalone ₹65.2668 crore, five actual intervals, CAGR **26.64%**. This yields REV's starting score 8, no trajectory adjustment, capped at 8 for Medium confidence. The three-year FY23 trough-to-FY26 CAGR is **71.17%** and is shown, not chosen because it is larger. A ten-year CAGR is ND because eleven annual observations are unavailable for this 2017-incorporated issuer. [AR21] [AR22] [AR23] [AR26]\\n\\n| Period | Parent operating revenue | Change / interpretation |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| FY19 comparative | 3.2119785 | Early small base; not a mature-cycle anchor. |\\n| FY20 | 13.1965046 | Original statutory accounts. |\\n| FY21 | 20.0357211 | Five-year CAGR start. |\\n| FY22 | 15.8384182 | Contraction, not uninterrupted compounding. |\\n| FY23 | 13.0138330 | Another contraction; trough exaggerates subsequent CAGR. |\\n| FY24 | 34.0058580 | Addition ₹20.992025 crore; FY23 consolidated restatement versus FY24 standalone is not a strictly organic consolidated bridge. |\\n| FY25 | 51.1154640 | Addition ₹17.109606 crore, +50.31%. |\\n| FY26 | 65.2668000 | Addition ₹14.151336 crore, +27.69%. New terminal subsidiary revenue zero. |\\n\\nOriginal FY20–FY24 revenue is preserved separately from restated earnings/cash vintages. Former subsidiaries were divested in FY24; growth of the continuing parent is identifiable, but **price, throughput, new-site, consulting mix and pass-through costs are not fully decomposed**. The new terminal did not manufacture FY26 consolidated revenue growth. Historical former-subsidiary disposal, acquisitions, organic volume and headline percentage growth must not be conflated. [AR24] [IPO] [AR26]\\n\\nH1 FY26 operating revenue was **₹30.4889 crore**, +37.85% on ₹22.1173 crore in H1 FY25; management's ₹30.72 crore referred to **total income**, not operating revenue. H1 PAT was ₹3.2268 crore. FY26 less H1 gives H2 revenue ₹34.7779 crore and PAT ₹3.3546 crore. This is a same-year decomposition, not a new TTM. No H1 FY27 result was located by the cutoff; do not incorporate later November results or annualize one half-year mechanically. [HY25] [AR26]\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nGrowth access is credible but not free. The **₹9.6 crore, three-year Deepak Phenolics contract** has an average annual equivalent of ₹3.2 crore, roughly 4.9% of FY26 parent revenue. It is not automatically wholly incremental to existing revenue. Management's **₹240 crore backlog at 27 November 2025** covered the remaining execution of existing contracts over approximately 35–36 months, not ₹240 crore of newly won business. A ₹60–70 crore first-year execution indication is more informative than dividing backlog into an assumed windfall. Opening backlog, additions, execution, cancellations and margins are not reconciled by cutoff. [DEEPAK] [CALL25, pp3–4][CALL25]\\n\\nA bottom-up scaling check: on the model's analytical base entry run-rate of ₹77.014824 crore, 12% annual growth requires approximately ₹9.24 crore first-year additional revenue, almost three Deepak-sized annual equivalents. The bull entry run-rate is ₹81.5835 crore and 20% growth requires ₹16.32 crore of first-year additions, about five such equivalents. These comparisons test scale; they are not a forecast of five identical contracts. They require staffing, customer qualification, vendor mobilization and working capital, with no verified capacity/utilization schedule that can deliver them automatically. [A; model_inputs.json]\\n\\n**Project status ladder:**\\n\\n- Existing O&M and consulting: operating and revenue-generating, with disclosed concentration and collection risk.\\n- IPO equipment/software: partly acquired; substantial allocations remained unspent at 31 March 2026. Packing system allocation was unspent. Cost saving from replacing rentals remains a management thesis until realized net of depreciation, maintenance and financing. [AR26] [CALL25]\\n- Repono Terminal: acquired/controlled in November 2025; reported FY26 turnover zero; parent loan ₹3.0995 crore, group CWIP ₹3.7890 crore. Not mature terminal earnings. [AR26]\\n- Mathura step-down: incorporated 8 April 2026 with ₹1 lakh share subscription; Reliance arrangement announced 30 May. Cost, capacity, tariff, committed debt/equity, guarantees and complete parent recourse are not quantified in the filing. [MATHURA26] [RIL26]\\n- Railway ESP approval: a 30 September company-issued syndicated announcement describes a 20-year RIL arrangement and a target of operations within 36 months **of EPC commencement**, with formalities still needed before construction. The BSE attachment itself was inaccessible. This is M, not an independently read regulatory approval or proof of commissioning. [RAIL26]\\n- Saudi Repono GW: 51% interest announced in February 2026, formed January 2026; annual wording/perimeter is inconsistent. No underwritten revenue or terminal value is assigned. A separate ₹0.1362 crore formation advance in the parent notes concerns **UAE**, not automatically Saudi. [SAUDI26] [AR26]\\n\\nThe November 2025 call explicitly anticipated funding the terminal with **cash accruals, new debt and probably equity**. That is a financing warning, not a commitment from lenders or investors. Its contemporaneous explanation of other noncurrent assets as equipment advances is management commentary, not a reconciliation of FY26 FD/CFO classifications. [CALL25, pp6–8][CALL25]\\n\\nThe model charges finite IPO spending and ₹3–6 crore parent project contributions over the first three investment years, with **zero new-terminal/Saudi value**. Those contributions are bounded analyst stresses, **not** estimates of total project capex or maximum legal exposure. A truly large project could require much more. CAP remains NS despite computable aggregate proxies; CASH=3 imposes the GQS cap, while the missing CAP input keeps GQS NS.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\nPromoters held **72.47%** at 31 March 2026, with no pledge, non-disposal undertaking or other encumbrance reported then. The two main founders' combined holding is not the whole promoter-group percentage. Director remuneration was ₹1.20 crore, about 18.2% of parent PAT; related rent and related loans are disclosed and warrant commercial scrutiny, not an automatic allegation. Loans to Repono Green remain material after disposal of the ownership stake. No new warrant/ESOP/convertible claim was identified in the reviewed records; that is not a guarantee of absence after the cutoff. [SHP26] [AR26] [IPO]\\n\\n| Dated statement / commitment | Outcome by cutoff | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| November 2025: FY26 revenue around ₹65–66 crore | Operating revenue ₹65.2668 crore; total income ₹66.4532 crore | Broad revenue delivery, but definitions matter. [CALL25] [AR26] |\\n| November 2025: large terminal award expected by December / 30–45 days | RIL arrangement announced May 2026 | Milestone achieved later; not yet an operating-return record. [CALL25] [RIL26] |\\n| IPO objects planned in FY26 | ₹16.6535 crore earmarked balance at March 2026, including all packing allocation | Deployment delayed; disclosed ability to use subsequent years is not proof of diversion or of execution. [AR26] [IPO] |\\n| November 2025: leased MHE/forklift purchases should reduce costs | Equipment acquired, but equipment rent still ₹6.8763 crore and large pallet/furniture additions | Require like-for-like rental savings after maintenance/depreciation; no automatic margin uplift. [CALL25] [AR26] |\\n| November 2025: first Saudi business hoped for by March 2026 | Incorporation disclosed; audited revenue/perimeter not reconciled | Incorporation is not commercial delivery. [CALL25] [SAUDI26] [AR26] |\\n\\n**Full audit review:** Original FY20–FY23 main reports, CARO and IFC annexures were read; opinions were unmodified and IFC positive. FY24 main/CARO was read, with IFC **exempt**, not an omitted favorable opinion. Its KAM covers divestments of Repono Green 99.90% and Enerjaa 50% at face value above valuation. The previous auditor resigned on 15 April 2024 and V S Bapna & Associates was appointed. Do not claim an unchanged auditor history; a disclosed resignation alone proves no misconduct. Original FY25 annual audit was not recovered, so the offer restatement and FY26 comparative do **not** substitute for a falsely claimed full FY25 CARO/IFC review. FY26 complete standalone and consolidated main/CARO/IFC were read. `audit_evidence.json` records the coverage and exceptions. [AR20] [AR21] [AR22] [AR23] [AR24] [IPO] [AR26]\\n\\nFY26 audits are unmodified with positive IFC/audit-trail statements and no reported default, willful defaulter, fraud or one-year material uncertainty. The export receivable KAM, negative NCI, cash-flow classifications and proceeds mapping nevertheless require substantive reconciliation. Smaller CARO template contradictions, including the audit-committee wording, are retained proportionately. **G1 Conditional, not Fail; G2 Hold, not a fraud finding.**\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\n### Historical statements and restatement boundaries\\n\\n| Restated/current vintage | Revenue | PAT | Reported CFO | Cash capex | Gross debt | Cash equivalents | Equity | Scope |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| FY23 | 13.0138 | 0.5201 | -1.0319 | 1.5895 | 1.5250 | 0.2834 | 4.8658 | Consolidated former subsidiaries |\\n| FY24 | 34.0059 | 4.1822 | -0.0262 | 2.0693 | 3.5380 | 0.0507 | 9.0694 | Standalone |\\n| FY25 | 51.1155 | 5.1490 | 0.3296 | 1.4692 | 6.1325 | 0.0584 | 14.2185 | Standalone |\\n| FY26 | 65.2668 | 6.5814 | -18.2300 | 8.8755 | 9.1252 | 0.0337 | 44.5955 | Standalone |\\n\\nFY23 above is consolidated with former subsidiaries, FY24–FY25 are offer-document standalone restatements, and FY26 is current standalone statutory. Do not sum this entire series and call it a single unchanged consolidation perimeter. FY26 consolidated revenue ₹65.2668 crore, total PAT ₹6.5782 crore, owner PAT ₹6.5783 crore, CFO **−₹15.1323 crore**, cash capex ₹11.8892 crore, cash ₹0.1262 crore and gross debt ₹9.1342 crore are separately retained. Consolidated reported owner equity ₹45.1463 crore and NCI **−₹0.5595 crore** require reconciliation, not an automatic valuation benefit. [IPO, F7–F9][IPO] [AR26]\\n\\n| Original standalone vintage | Revenue | PAT | CFO | Cash capex | Cash equivalents | Equity |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY20 | 13.1965 | 1.2168 | 0.3531 | 0.0987 | 0.3587 | 1.2979 |\\n| FY21 | 20.0357 | 1.6178 | 4.4409 | 2.8307 | 0.1866 | 2.9157 |\\n| FY22 | 15.8384 | 1.4536 | 0.1621 | 0.4313 | 2.4651 | 4.3693 |\\n| FY23 | 13.0138 | 0.6242 | -1.0428 | 1.4095 | 0.2597 | 4.9935 |\\n| FY24 | 34.0059 | 4.0342 | 0.4359 | 2.2493 | 0.0507 | 9.0277 |\\n\\nOriginal statements use rupees in FY20–FY22 and thousands in FY23–FY24. Conversion is explicit in the inputs. FY24 original PAT ₹4.034215 crore becomes restated ₹4.182163 crore through gratuity +₹0.078989 crore and deferred tax +₹0.068959 crore. FY25 book PAT ₹5.100374 crore becomes restated ₹5.149041 crore through deferred tax +₹0.048667 crore. FY23 original standalone PAT and restated consolidated/owner PAT differ by both perimeter and adjustments. These are retained, not smoothed away. [AR20] [AR21] [AR22] [AR23] [AR24] [IPO, F21/F27][IPO]\\n\\nA further historical classification bridge is explicit: original FY23 CFO −₹1.042773 crore becomes −₹1.375684 crore in FY24's comparative, offset exactly by +₹0.325 crore financing and +₹0.007911 crore investing interest. Debt/AP shift by ₹0.325 crore; closing cash is unchanged. Original FY24 CFO is ₹0.435858 crore, verified from the page image rather than an OCR misread. [AR23] [AR24]\\n\\nFY26's FY25 comparative reports PAT ₹5.1004 crore, CFO **−₹1.3705 crore**, capex ₹1.1195 crore and equity ₹14.1281 crore, rather than the offer-restated figures. The CFO difference of **₹1.70011 crore** is largely offset by investing **₹1.698971 crore** and financing **₹0.001127 crore** in the opposite direction. FD/current investment ₹0.997855 crore, other noncurrent ₹0.351419 crore and PPE ₹0.349678 crore largely explain the investing movement. **Closing cash reconciles; cash is not alleged missing.** Detailed classifications and restatement carry-forward are still insufficiently explained for G2 clearance. [IPO, F9][IPO] [AR26, p72][AR26]\\n\\n### Earnings to cash, without a false capex excuse\\n\\nFY26 parent operating EBIT = PBT ₹9.1715 + finance ₹0.9820 − other income ₹1.1864 = **₹8.9671 crore**. Add depreciation ₹0.9976 for operating EBITDA **₹9.9647 crore**, or 15.27% of operating revenue. An EBITDA measure including other income is ₹11.1511 crore; it is not the same operating denominator. The ₹1.17 crore cancelled-contract export reversal is not an expense that can simply be added back. No normalized PAT claim is made beyond the transparent operating-earnings bridge. [AR26]\\n\\nReported FY26 parent CFO is **−₹18.2300 crore**. The source's rounded component lines sum to −₹18.2299 crore, a ₹0.0001 crore rounding difference. Important uses include receivables ₹3.6360 crore, short loans ₹0.4639 crore, subsidiary loan ₹3.0995 crore, other current assets ₹2.5204 crore and other noncurrent assets ₹17.1141 crore. Cash capex is in **investing**, not a reason by itself for weak CFO. The noncurrent-asset movement includes FD increases, deposit release and unamortized expenditure movements. [AR26, p72 and notes14/18/19][AR26]\\n\\n| Clearly labeled diagnostic | ₹ crore | What it does **not** establish |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| Reported CFO | −18.2300 | Not all operating deterioration. |\\n| Add exact FD increase 19.6766−1.3768 | +18.2998 | FDs are not automatically available cash. |\\n| CFO after FD reclassification only | +0.0698 | Not a complete clean operating CFO. |\\n| Also remove separately identified parent-to-subsidiary loan | +3.0995 | Group financing still consumes shareholder capital. |\\n| FD/subsidiary-loan adjusted diagnostic | +3.1693 | Other loans, IPO/tax advances and noncash issuance-asset movements remain. |\\n| Less cash finance cost classified in financing | −0.9820 | Diagnostic after financing charge = ₹2.1873 crore, not owner FCF. |\\n\\nSecurity deposits declined **₹0.2444 crore**; miscellaneous expenditure not written off declined **₹0.9413 crore**. The latter may reflect issue-cost treatment, not a cash receipt. H1 note6 capitalized IPO expenses ₹2.5135108 crore, and FY26 note3 deducts ₹2.7943 crore from securities premium; no specific reconciliation establishes that the ₹0.9413 crore movement is cash. Therefore the ₹3.1693 crore diagnostic is **not called normalized CFO**. [HY25] [AR26]\\n\\nPre-IPO FY24–FY25 restated parent cumulative CFO **₹0.303426 crore / PAT ₹9.331204 crore = 3.25%**. FY20–FY24 original parent cumulative conversion is 48.61%; FY21's strong CFO contains a ₹1.6721623 crore increase in other long-term liabilities that reverses in FY22. FY22 CFO is only ₹0.1620913 crore, visually checked, while a ₹1.8 crore investment withdrawal helps cash. FY24–FY26 reported cumulative conversion is −112.66%, but that is heavily distorted by FY26 FD/loan classification and is not the sole basis for CASH=3. [AR20] [AR21] [AR22] [AR23] [AR24] [IPO] [AR26]\\n\\nReceivables rose ₹11.7467→₹15.3827 crore. Balances over six months rose **₹0.2306→₹2.5422 crore**; the FY26 ageing schedule includes ₹2.0387 crore at six–twelve months, ₹0.4354 crore at one–two years and smaller older balances. Average-receivable days on total-revenue proxy rise from about 65.73 in FY25 to 75.86 in FY26. Closing-balance days are not confused with this average. Inventory and payable days have incompatible service-cost denominators; the model's inventory/revenue and 38%-of-sales payable-cost proxies are labeled assumptions, not audited cash-conversion-cycle statistics. [AR26] [IPO]\\n\\n### Capital returns and funding bridge\\n\\n| Measure | FY24 | FY25 | FY26 | Limitation |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| Operating EBITDA, excluding other income | 6.0432 | 8.1331 | 9.9647 | Restated/current vintage mix. |\\n| Standard-tax NOPAT, 27.82% assumption | 4.1455 | 5.2817 | 6.4725 | Analytical tax normalization, not cash tax. |\\n| Core trade-plus-fixed IC | 10.9558 | 17.4181 | 28.1437 | AR+inventory−AP+PPE+intangibles+CWIP; not full ONWC. |\\n| NOPAT / average core IC | 48.58% | 37.23% | 28.41% | FY24 opening perimeter differs; proxy, not full ROIC. |\\n| Operating EBIT / average equity plus gross debt (ROCE) | 60.46% | 44.40% | 24.21% | Defined financing-denominator ratio; retains idle IPO cash, unlike core operating IC. |\\n| Incremental NOPAT / incremental core IC | 74.28% | 17.58% | 11.10% | FY24 trough/perimeter distortion; FY26 projects have not matured. |\\n| Reported CFO less cash capex | −2.0955 | −1.1396 | −27.1055 | FD/loan classification matters; cash interest still separate. |\\n\\nFY25–FY26 incremental numerator is **₹1.1907152 crore NOPAT**, denominator **₹10.725603 crore core IC**, giving 11.10%, not evidence of attractive returns on all new capital. FY26 selected operating IC adds signed other operating assets/liabilities **−₹1.0194 crore**, reaching ₹27.1243 crore. Components are supplier advances, prepaids, employee advances, security deposits and GST input, less salaries/vendor/statutory employee payables, gratuity and expense provisions. The financing-side bridge is ₹27.4317 crore after excluding cash, FDs, nonoperating advances and the subsidiary equity investment, leaving an explicit **₹0.3074 crore unresolved tax/other classification residual**. It is not forced to zero. Full historical cohort economics and project cost are missing; CAP remains NS. [AR26, notes6–19][AR26]\\n\\nThe major asset addition deserves scrutiny: ₹5.35 crore wooden pallets were purchased in Maharashtra and transferred to Gujarat; furniture/fixture additions were ₹5.9402 crore. Auditor notes cite management's documentation representation, not an independent future-useful-life guarantee. New-year depreciation is not an appropriate automatic maintenance-capex estimate. The forward model tests maintenance at 1–4% of revenue. [AR26, PPE schedule][AR26]\\n\\n**Cash and debt cannot be netted indiscriminately.** Parent cash equivalents are ₹0.0337 crore, debt ₹9.1252 crore, including ICICI CC ₹8.4961 crore and vehicle debt ₹0.6291 crore. FDs total ₹19.6766 crore, of which ₹16.6535 crore is earmarked IPO and ₹3.0231 crore has no verified free-cash/lien map. Cash finance cost ₹0.9820 crore comprises interest ₹0.5446 crore, processing ₹0.3728 crore and bank-guarantee charges ₹0.0646 crore. Current coverage of EBIT/total finance cost is about 9.13×; favorable accounting coverage does not prove renewal liquidity. Bank guarantees ₹4.8425 crore and GST dispute ₹0.2460 crore are contingencies, not both cash spent or both zero risk. [AR26]\\n\\nIPO gross proceeds were **₹26.68032 crore**, net cash equity proceeds **₹23.8861 crore** after issue expenses. CARO's object table shows spent ₹10.02682 crore and remaining ₹16.6535 crore: equipment ₹4.7730, packing ₹1.6048, software ₹0.6962, working capital ₹9.5795. GCP allocation ₹6.67 crore is shown spent. These gross-object allocations and the separate issue-cost deduction need a gross/net reconciliation; do not count gross proceeds and net proceeds as two sources. The exchange proceeds form also contains a malformed amount/units presentation and incomplete object detail. These are reconciliation questions, not a proven use-of-proceeds violation. [AR26, CARO][AR26] [PROCEEDS26]\\n\\nThe rounded statutory cash bridge is: opening ₹0.0584 − CFO ₹18.2300 − capex ₹8.8755 − subsidiary equity ₹0.0010 + interest received ₹1.0687 + rent ₹0.1175 + net issue ₹23.8861 − term debt ₹0.4636 + short debt ₹3.4552 − financing cash ₹0.9820 ≈ closing **₹0.0337 crore**, with ₹0.0001 crore rounding residual. There is no dividend double count. Bonuses in FY21/FY23/FY25 are not economic dilution; the 2,779,200 new IPO shares increased pre-IPO 7,500,000 shares by 37.056%. Reported FY26 weighted-average EPS is ₹7.06 on 9,319,804.93 weighted shares. Current-share FY26 parent EPS is **₹6.4026**, versus restated FY25 ₹6.8654 on the post-bonus 7.5 million base, a decline of about 6.74%, despite total PAT growth. [AR26] [IPO]\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\n### Cross-check, not a price target\\n\\nAt the conditional ₹73.90 reference, current-share parent P/E is **11.54×**, parent EV/operating EBITDA **8.54×**, EV/sales 1.30× and P/B 1.70×. EV = ₹75.963288 crore market value + ₹9.1252 crore debt − **only** ₹0.0337 crore cash = ₹85.054788 crore. No restricted FD or unrecovered related loan is subtracted; subsidiary/project value is not invented. Consolidated EV is NS because the NCI/perimeter bridge is unresolved. Reported FCF yield is deeply negative and classification-distorted; it is not a clean owner-earnings yield. These multiples are compatible with both undervaluation and a financing/value trap. [C from AR26/IPO and market reference; AR26] [IPO] [MARKET19]\\n\\n### Cash-first model and explicit assumptions\\n\\nThe model starts at **audited 31 March 2026**, not a fabricated October balance. Seven pre-entry periods total 202 days through 19 October; then 60 equal monthly periods and 12 actual continuation-model months. Every operating cycle pays cash costs before collection, with eight cycles per month by default. A ₹0.02 crore operating floor is checked after **every event**. There is no return after the first breach, even if later projected earnings would be positive. Failure in this counterfactual model is **not a claim the issuer actually defaulted in April 2026**. The missing actual bank bridge is precisely why R is NS.\\n\\n| A: assumption | Bear | Base | Bull |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| Pre-entry annual run-rate uplift over FY26 | 8% | 18% | 25% |\\n| Five-year annual revenue growth | −3% | 12% | 20% |\\n| Operating EBITDA margin | 8% | 14.5% | 17% |\\n| Receivable-days target | 120 | 90 | 75 |\\n| Inventory / sales days proxy | 3 | 2 | 2 |\\n| Payable days on 38%-of-sales cost proxy | 18 | 28 | 35 |\\n| Other signed operating capital / sales | +1.5% | −1.0% | −1.5% |\\n| Parent project contributions, first three years | ₹6 crore | ₹3 crore | ₹3 crore |\\n\\nShared assumptions: tax 27.82%; depreciation 1.53% of sales; maintenance capex 2% of sales; growth fixed-capital investment 15% of incremental annual revenue; other working balances converge over months rather than jumping for free; vehicle principal ₹0.1986 crore per year; CC ₹8.4961 crore repaid in investment month five with **no automatic rollover**. That date is a stress assumption, not a disclosed maturity. Existing debt cash charge 10%; separately optional new debt costs 14%, capped ₹2 crore and fully repaid at year five. Optional debt upfront fee is assumed **zero**, so gross draw equals net cash; this is unverified and optimistic, unlike the separately charged 3% equity issue cost. No dividend is paid and then also retained in equity value.\\n\\n**Funding modes:**\\n\\n1. **Strict verified-only:** no FD release, new equity or new debt before/through entry. All three cases breach the floor at the first modeled operating payment; no ordinary return. This demonstrates the need for a reconciled treasury bridge, not an actual insolvency conclusion.\\n2. **Purpose-matched conditional release:** existing IPO equipment/packing/software pools may be released only against matching cash capex; WC pool only against eligible operating payment shortfalls. Pools are finite. No tax, debt repayment or unrelated project is funded directly from an earmark. Availability, liens and release timing remain **unverified**, so this is not committed finance.\\n3. **Optional rescue after a successful pre-entry bridge only:** whole-share equity at a 25% discount to each modeled entry price, 3% issue costs, capped at the lower of authorized headroom **1,220,800 shares** and ₹7 crore net proceeds. Authorization headroom is not board/shareholder/regulatory approval or investor willingness. The discounted price is a stress input, not a conclusion about legally permissible issue pricing. A separate ₹2 crore debt sensitivity is not assumed bank sanction. At ₹73.90, maximum net equity is ₹6.56329548 crore and final shares 11,500,000. No rescue is allowed to repair a pre-entry breach retroactively.\\n\\n| Scenario | FD policy | Optional new shares | Optional new debt, ₹ crore | Outcome / five-year CAGR if earned |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| bear | purpose_matched | 0 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 7.8637 |\\n| bear | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 2.3799 |\\n| bear | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 2 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 0.4641 |\\n| base | purpose_matched | 0 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 7.0753 |\\n| base | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 0.5120 |\\n| base | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 2 | 4.79% |\\n| bull | purpose_matched | 0 | 0 | NS: investment/Y1M05 cash_credit_repayment_no_rollover; shortfall 3.5795 |\\n| bull | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 0 | 17.86% |\\n| bull | purpose_matched | 1220800 | 2 | 17.74% |\\n\\nMinimum whole-share survival searches retain every trial and the one-share-lower failure. Survival includes year-six funding, not merely positive year-five cash.\\n\\n| Scenario | Optional debt | Minimum new whole shares | Result | Conditional CAGR |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|---:|\\n| bear | 0 | No feasible rescue | NO_FEASIBLE_RESCUE_WITHIN_CAP | NS |\\n| bear | 2 | No feasible rescue | NO_FEASIBLE_RESCUE_WITHIN_CAP | NS |\\n| base | 0 | No feasible rescue | NO_FEASIBLE_RESCUE_WITHIN_CAP | NS |\\n| base | 2 | 959693 | CONDITIONAL_MINIMUM_WHOLE_SHARE_RESCUE | 5.14% |\\n| bull | 0 | 665808 | CONDITIONAL_MINIMUM_WHOLE_SHARE_RESCUE | 18.77% |\\n| bull | 2 | 309464 | CONDITIONAL_MINIMUM_WHOLE_SHARE_RESCUE | 19.19% |\\n\\nMore equity can lower per-share returns even when it improves cash survival. No case is called attractive solely because optional rescue makes an equation solvent.\\n\\n### Annual linked cash paths\\n\\nBelow are annual totals for the maximum bounded equity plus ₹2 crore optional-debt cases, under the conditional FD policy. Infeasible cases stop at their breach; missing years are **not zero or reconstructed ordinary returns**. Every monthly balance, partial failed period, operating collection/payment, earmark, financing event and debt/share balance is retained in the machine-readable full paths.\\n\\n| Case | Year | Revenue | EBITDA | ΔONWC | CFO after cash finance | Capex | Project cash | CC repayment | Term principal | Optional debt repayment | Closing cash |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| base | 1 | 86.2566 | 12.5072 | 2.7027 | 6.2036 | 7.5754 | 1.0000 | 8.4961 | 0.1986 | 0.0000 | 2.9571 |\\n| base | 2 | 96.6074 | 14.0081 | 2.2597 | 8.0439 | 3.4848 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1986 | 0.0000 | 6.3177 |\\n| base | 3 | 108.2003 | 15.6890 | 2.4448 | 9.1349 | 3.9029 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1220 | 0.0000 | 10.4276 |\\n| base | 4 | 121.1843 | 17.5717 | 2.7285 | 10.2685 | 4.3713 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 16.3248 |\\n| base | 5 | 135.7264 | 19.6803 | 3.0549 | 11.5260 | 4.8958 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 2.0000 | 20.9550 |\\n| bull | 1 | 97.9002 | 16.6430 | 2.3686 | 9.5725 | 8.8696 | 1.0000 | 8.4961 | 0.1986 | 0.0000 | 7.5773 |\\n| bull | 2 | 117.4802 | 19.9716 | 3.0388 | 11.6581 | 5.2866 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1986 | 0.0000 | 12.7503 |\\n| bull | 3 | 140.9763 | 23.9660 | 3.6685 | 14.0250 | 6.3439 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1220 | 0.0000 | 19.3093 |\\n| bull | 4 | 169.1715 | 28.7592 | 4.4047 | 16.8716 | 7.6127 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 28.5682 |\\n| bull | 5 | 203.0059 | 34.5110 | 5.2859 | 20.2861 | 9.1353 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 2.0000 | 37.7190 |\\n\\n### Earned terminal economics and claim bridge\\n\\nOnly a path that funds all five years is allowed to attempt a continuation year. Year six assumes 3.5% growth, a 15% capital charge and an incremental-return ceiling of 15%. It pays actual operating capital and maintenance/growth capex, plus extra economic reinvestment if needed so growth is not free: required net reinvestment ≥ NOPAT×growth/15%. **No new financing or FD releases are permitted during that continuation year.** Terminal FCFF equals NOPAT minus actual net reinvestment, and EV = FCFF/(capital charge−growth). This is a conditional capitalization of parent earnings, not a liquidating value or a perpetual high-growth multiple.\\n\\nA cash reserve equal to the worst continuation-year intra-period drawdown plus the floor is retained. Only cash above that reserve at year five is added to EV; outstanding debt is deducted once, current diluted shares divide equity once, and earmarked FDs, related loans, UAE/Saudi/Mathura value are not added. Terminal operating value is 100% of the operating exit-value calculation, with no interim distributions: substantial duration/terminal sensitivity remains even after the funding test. Static hurdle-entry prices in terminal records hold financing constant and are labeled diagnostics; the reverse-price searches below **reprice the rescue and recompute the whole path**.\\n\\n### Diagnostic exit-multiple normalization\\n\\nThe intrinsic, reinvestment-funded FCFF calculation remains primary. The following **8× / 10× / 12× year-five operating EBITDA** checks hold each already-surviving cash path fixed, retain exactly the same required operating reserve, add only the same excess year-five cash, deduct outstanding debt once and divide by actual diluted shares. This is a valuation-normalization diagnostic, not a preferred rerating assumption, a new funding source or an earned intrinsic price target. No failed path receives an exit price or return: all failed cases remain null in `multiple_normalization.json`. Parent operating EBITDA excludes other income, as in the historical analysis. All rows below require the maximum 1,220,800-share optional issue; optional debt is shown separately. Cash/earnings amounts are ₹ crore and exit prices are ₹ per share.\\n\\n| Conditional case | Optional new debt | Exit EV/EBITDA | Year-five EBITDA | Required reserve | Excess cash added | Debt deducted | Diluted shares | Diagnostic exit price | Diagnostic five-year CAGR |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| base | 2 | 8 | 19.6803 | 1.2665 | 19.6885 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 154.03 | 15.82% |\\n| base | 2 | 10 | 19.6803 | 1.2665 | 19.6885 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 188.25 | 20.56% |\\n| base | 2 | 12 | 19.6803 | 1.2665 | 19.6885 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 222.48 | 24.66% |\\n| bull | 0 | 8 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 36.9038 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 272.17 | 29.79% |\\n| bull | 0 | 10 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 36.9038 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 332.19 | 35.07% |\\n| bull | 0 | 12 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 36.9038 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 392.20 | 39.63% |\\n| bull | 2 | 8 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 35.8932 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 271.29 | 29.71% |\\n| bull | 2 | 10 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 35.8932 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 331.31 | 34.99% |\\n| bull | 2 | 12 | 34.5110 | 1.8258 | 35.8932 | 0.0000 | 11500000 | 391.33 | 39.57% |\\n\\nA multiple does not pay maintenance capex, working capital or creditors. Attractive results in this table cannot override an intrinsic return shortfall, unavailable funding, the year-six cash proof, or unresolved gates. The machine-readable records retain the intrinsic implied EV/EBITDA alongside each diagnostic multiple for comparison.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations, funding boundaries and sensitivities\\n\\n| Conditional case / variable | Equity hurdle | Feasible root bracket |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| base price | 12.00% | No feasible bracket on grid |\\n| base price | 15.00% | No feasible bracket on grid |\\n| base price | 18.00% | No feasible bracket on grid |\\n| bull price | 12.00% | 97.034686–97.034688 |\\n| bull price | 15.00% | 83.862441–83.862443 |\\n| bull price | 18.00% | 73.065362–73.065364 |\\n| base growth | 15.00% | No feasible bracket on grid |\\n| base margin | 15.00% | 0.203076–0.203076 |\\n\\nEach reverse request retains all scan and bisection paths, including failed ones. Feasibility-transition boundaries are separately bisected; no root is interpolated across an insolvent interval. No feasible bracket means **none found on the declared domain**, not a global theorem. Base-case price scans cover ₹20–120, growth 0–50%, and margin 8–24%; bull price scans cover ₹20–150. The maximum whole-share issue is repriced at each trial price, subject to both caps. At the ₹73.90 reference and base growth/working-capital settings, the conditional 15% hurdle requires an operating EBITDA margin around **20.31%**, well above FY26's 15.27%, with optional funding still required. Raising growth alone can destroy feasibility before achieving the hurdle. In the declared base price domain, the first feasible boundary is about ₹58.09408 and the conditional return there is about 9.82%, below even the 12% hurdle. The base growth feasibility boundary is approximately 23.9294%, where return is about 12.50%; higher growth breaches cash before reaching 15%. These are local modeled boundaries, not global impossibility claims.\\n\\n| Two-way test: growth / margin, or terminal capital charge / growth | Conditional result |\\n|---|---:|\\n| growth_margin/.08/.10 | -9.78% |\\n| growth_margin/.08/.145 | 2.40% |\\n| growth_margin/.08/.17 | 6.97% |\\n| growth_margin/.08/.20 | 11.63% |\\n| growth_margin/.12/.10 | Cash breach; no return |\\n| growth_margin/.12/.145 | 4.79% |\\n| growth_margin/.12/.17 | 9.64% |\\n| growth_margin/.12/.20 | 14.54% |\\n| growth_margin/.20/.10 | Cash breach; no return |\\n| growth_margin/.20/.145 | 9.85% |\\n| growth_margin/.20/.17 | 15.18% |\\n| growth_margin/.20/.20 | 20.58% |\\n| growth_margin/.30/.10 | Cash breach; no return |\\n| growth_margin/.30/.145 | Cash breach; no return |\\n| growth_margin/.30/.17 | 22.50% |\\n| growth_margin/.30/.20 | 28.46% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.12/.02 | 22.69% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.12/.035 | 23.84% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.12/.05 | 25.31% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.15/.02 | 17.47% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.15/.035 | 17.74% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.15/.05 | 18.01% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.18/.02 | 13.65% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.18/.035 | 13.48% |\\n| terminal_k_g/.18/.05 | 13.19% |\\n\\nThe full one-way tests additionally vary receivable days 60–120, maintenance 1–4% of sales, CC repayment months 3/5/12/60, project contributions ₹0/3/6/12 crore, cash cycles 4/8/16/32, existing borrowing cost 8–14%, cash floor ₹0.01/0.02/0.05 crore and growth fixed-capital intensity 10/15/25%. Each recomputes cash, tax, capital, debt, dilution and terminal viability, not merely terminal P/E. ₹0 project cash is an optional-project exclusion sensitivity, not proof the contract costs nothing. Execution charges/impact are not estimable from the available tape; a 2% combined entry/exit friction would further reduce a five-year CAGR by roughly 0.4 percentage points, without repairing a funding failure. **No supported buy-price range is issued.**\\n\\n### Liquidity implementation\\n\\nThe official master verifies 1,200 shares per lot. Secondary 19 October data show five trades and ₹437,304 turnover; deliverable quantity 6,000 is **not total traded volume**. Only 18 observed dates are available, not a complete 20/60-session sample. The sample-only 5% participation estimate is 5.49 days for ₹5 lakh, with 2% and 10% alternatives in `liquidity.json`. Missing dates are not inserted as zero and this diagnostic does not earn an L score. Actual exit may be dominated by one-sided circuits, spread, impact and lot granularity. G4 remains Hold. [MASTER19] [MARKET19]\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\n**Impairment risk: High, with uncertain loss severity, not a quantified probability.** The decisive variable is whether customer collections and available, legally usable liquidity fund obligations before cash is due. The linked loss path is: a concentrated customer is lost or disputed → utilization and margins fall → aged receivables cannot fund payroll/vendor payments → CC is not renewed while project contributions continue → discounted equity is needed or unavailable → dilution, asset sales or claim impairment fall on existing shareholders. A large accounting FD balance does not interrupt this sequence if earmarked or pledged. [IPO] [AR26; A stress model]\\n\\nThe strongest contrary evidence to the cautious interpretation is real revenue growth, profitable current contracts, a low gross-debt/operating-EBITDA ratio of about 0.92×, unmodified audits and earmarked assets that may in practice be readily releasable. If bank evidence confirms that access and a credible terminal budget is fully financed, the strict case is too pessimistic. Conversely, promoter holding, brand-name customers, railway design approval and ₹240 crore management backlog do not by themselves resolve cash claims.\\n\\nThe bear cash path fails within the modeled horizon even at the bounded optional issue/debt assumptions. **An ordinary bear target and CAGR are withheld after breach**; neither a negative bank balance nor zero is used as a fake share price. Liquidation recovery is ND without enforceable collateral rank, guarantees, receivable recoveries and project obligations. A temporary quote decline differs from this permanent-loss mechanism; a lower quote alone does not repair G2/G3/G4.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\nA multibagger claim is **speculative / unsupported** at this stage. Five-times and ten-times share-price outcomes over five years require approximately 37.97% and 58.49% annual price growth. At unchanged net margin and P/E, a maximum modeled 11.88% increase in shares means revenue would need about 5.594× or 11.188× FY26, roughly **₹365 crore or ₹730 crore**. This arithmetic ignores the much harder problem of financing the capital, qualifying contracts, maintaining margins and protecting per-share claims. It is not a target or a success probability. A mature terminal multiple may differ from a service multiple, but no unsupported rerating is used to bridge that gap. [C from current shares/AR26; A unchanged economics]\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\nThis is a research cadence, not an automatically created reminder.\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Constructive evidence / milestone | Warning and thesis-break distinction | Review source / timing |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Export reversal and ageing | ₹1.17 crore reversal; >6-month AR ₹2.5422 crore | Cash receipts against identified old balances; reconciliation of cancelled contract | Warning: further ageing or reclassification; break: material recurring uncollectible revenue without credible recognition controls | Next results, ageing notes and auditor report |\\n| Treasury availability | Cash ₹0.0337 crore; earmarked FD ₹16.6535 crore | Bank-level maturities/liens and eligible object-release schedule | Warning: restricted cash used in headline net debt; break: evidenced payment default/unfinanceable essential operations | Facility and proceeds disclosures |\\n| CC renewal | ₹8.4961 crore CC | Signed sanction, maturity, covenant and undrawn-headroom terms | Warning: overdue renewal or increased collateral; break: withdrawal with no funded alternative | Bank/rating filings, annual notes |\\n| IPO objects and costs | Equipment4.7730, packing1.6048, software0.6962, WC9.5795 unspent | Objects-to-invoices-to-FD and gross/net issue-cost reconciliation | Warning: further unexplained delays; break: substantiated diversion, not delay alone | Half-year proceeds update |\\n| Mathura terminal | May agreement; September ESP announcement; no disclosed complete budget | Full capex, capacity, tariff, EPC start, committed financing and recourse | Warning: new parent guarantees before finance; break: binding unfunded obligation exceeding credible resources | Material disclosures before construction |\\n| Parent/subsidiary claims | Terminal100%, Saudi51% notice, negative NCI0.5595 | Auditor-backed consolidation and opening-equity reconciliation | Warning: further inconsistent ownership; break: material unreconciled misstatement or minority harm | Corrected accounts and subsidiary statements |\\n| Related loans | Green3.1357, Terminal3.0995 crore | Commercial terms, cash interest/principal collection, board approvals | Warning: growth without purpose/recovery; break: substantiated abusive allocation | RPT filings/notes each half-year |\\n| Contract economics | Top-five FY25 share82.45%; management backlog240 crore | Renewal/expiry schedule, executed backlog bridge, cash margin by cohort | Warning: price cuts or concentrated loss; break: sustained negative cash unit economics | Contract updates/results |\\n| Liquidity | Official lot1200; incomplete tape | Complete official20/60-series, restriction notices, executable two-way depth | Warning: one-sided circuits; break for new purchase: effectively untradeable position | Before any investment consideration |\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\n**Research priority / wait for evidence. Hold — unresolved.** This is not “buy because P/E is low,” not “avoid because SME,” and not a governance accusation. BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS; the record belongs in **pending evidence**, outside numerical rankings. It does not meet the compounder criteria, and the full classification matrix is overridden by unresolved gates rather than applied to invented midpoint scores.\\n\\nThe next decisive documents are: (1) the consolidation/NCI and cash-classification reconciliation; (2) a restricted-versus-available bank and proceeds map with CC renewal terms; (3) a complete, committed terminal funding and parent-recourse package; and (4) verified market implementation data. Better collections and demonstrated incremental cash returns would then allow CAP/BAL/R/P/L to be reconsidered. A different quote changes valuation inputs, not historical BQS/GQS. No trade is executed or position recommendation implied.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\n### Evidence and reconstruction ledger\\n\\n- **Primary audit/history:** original FY20–FY24 issuer annual reports, FY25 SEBI offer restatements and current FY26 report. Full applicable original audit annexures were reviewed; FY25 original remains a declared gap. Source dates, locators, hashes and per-claim limitations are in `source_register.json` and `audit_evidence.json`.\\n- **Current business/capital:** November 2025 call, September 2025 Deepak contract, February 2026 Saudi notice, April Mathura incorporation, May RIL arrangement, March shareholding and proceeds filings. Management forecasts remain M, not V outcomes.\\n- **Price/implementation:** official19October interop master for identity/shares/lot only; secondary dated market rows for the reference price and diagnostic turnover. No false official closing-price claim.\\n- **Analyst inputs:** every forecast and cash-timing assumption is declared in `model_inputs.json`; no terminal/Saudi revenue is hidden in the base forecast. No unsupported facility is treated as committed.\\n- **Canonical computation:** `build.py` and `model_engine.py` produce Decimal50 results, 325 unique full paths and 451 aliases, including all failed scenarios, reverse/root candidates and whole-share rescue trials. Deduplication is by full content hash, with no omitted events or decimal digits. `verify.py` independently checks event accounting, claims, caps, terminal reinvestment/reserve, scores and root/adjacent-share endpoints.\\n- **Recovery:** compact contains all authored minimum inputs and executable code; full also contains generated results. `python guarded_rebuild.py --clean` deletes generated outputs and prior attestations, forbids undeclared/external reads and network/process calls during rebuilding, then recreates and verifies them. Neither archive contains downloaded PDFs, OCR, third-party helper code, credentials or private file identifiers. Rebuild attestations demonstrate reproducibility, **not investment accuracy**.\\n\\nOpen questions are substantive, not empty placeholders: precise FY25 CFO reclassification and restatement carry-forward; NCI and foreign-subsidiary perimeter; the ₹0.9413 crore issuance-asset movement; IPO gross/net object treatment; FD liens and bank release terms; old receivable cash receipts; related-loan recoverability; project total budget, guarantees and funding; contracted minimum guarantees/termination rights; current ownership and tradability. Model conclusions remain conditional until these are answered.\\n\\n**Update log, cutoff 20 October 2026:** Initial v3.0 deep record. Corrected headline negative-CFO interpretation for FD classification; retained rather than erased contradictory fiscal vintages. Added full historical audit coverage, current official lot corroboration, whole-share optional funding and terminal cash proof. No prior score is silently carried forward. Retrieval/rebuild occurred after the cutoff, but no post-cutoff operating event is used.\\n\\n[AR20]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2019-2020.pdf\\n[AR21]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2020-2021.pdf\\n[AR22]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2021-2022.pdf\\n[AR23]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2022-2023.pdf\\n[AR24]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/2023-2024.pdf\\n[AR26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/Annual_Report/Repono_Annual_Report_2026_Final.pdf\\n[IPO]: https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/aug-2025/1754566211166_910.pdf\\n[HY25]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Outcome_of_the_Board_Meeting_13Nov25.pdf\\n[CALL25]: https://repono.in/H1-FY26-Concall-Transcript-Repono%20Limited_27Nov25.pdf\\n[SHP26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/Share_Holding_Pattern/SHP_31Mar26.pdf\\n[DEEPAK]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Intimation_for_awarding_of_order_or_contract_11Sep25.pdf\\n[SAUDI26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Acquisition_24Feb26.pdf\\n[MATHURA26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Acquisition_08Apr26.pdf\\n[RIL26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/SE_Disclosure_Regulation_30/Longterm_arrangement_agreement_with_Reliance_Industries_Ltd_30May26.pdf\\n[RAIL26]: https://companyvoice.in/repono-receives-railway-approval-for-engineering-scale-planesp-of-gati-shakti-cargo-petroleum-oil-terminal-at-ajhai-mathura/\\n[PROCEEDS26]: https://repono.in/annual-reports/fy2025-26/Statement_of_Derivatives_Variations/Statement_of_Derivatives_Variations_M26.pdf\\n[MASTER19]: https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz\\n[MARKET19]: https://www.indiainfoline.com/company/repono-ltd-historical-data\\n[AEGIS]: https://aegisindia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/ALL_SE_TranscriptEarningQ3_Signed.pdf\\n[AVTL]: https